window.BANKS=window.BANKS||{};window.BANKS["bp301t"]={"code":"BP301T","name":"Pharmaceutical Organic Chemistry II","units":{"1":"Benzene and its derivatives","2":"Phenols, aromatic amines and aromatic acids","3":"Fats and oils","4":"Polynuclear hydrocarbons","5":"Cycloalkanes"},"short":[{"id":"301S01","unit":1,"m":5,"q":"Give the analytical evidences in support of the structure of benzene.","hi":"C6H6, एक monosubstituted और तीन disubstituted isomer → symmetrical ring","kp":[{"t":"Molecular formula C6H6, highly unsaturated","k":["c6h6","unsaturat"]},{"t":"Gives substitution, not addition; no decolourisation of KMnO4/Br2 water","k":["substitution","does not decolor","not decolourise","bromine water"]},{"t":"Adds 3 H2 to form cyclohexane (ring with 3 double bonds)","k":["cyclohexane","three h2","3 h2","3 mol"]},{"t":"Only one monosubstituted derivative (all 6 H equivalent)","k":["one monosubstitut","only one mono","equivalent"]},{"t":"Three disubstituted isomers: ortho, meta, para","k":["three disubstitut","ortho meta para","o m p","3 isomers"]}],"a":"1. Elemental analysis and molecular weight give C6H6, showing high unsaturation.\n2. In spite of this, benzene does not decolourise bromine water or cold alkaline KMnO4; it prefers substitution over addition.\n3. With H2/Ni it adds 3 molecules of hydrogen giving cyclohexane, so it has a six-membered ring with three double bonds.\n4. It adds three molecules of Cl2 in sunlight (BHC) and gives triozonide (3 glyoxal) on ozonolysis.\n5. Only one monosubstituted product (e.g. C6H5Br) is formed, so all six H are equivalent.\n6. Three disubstituted isomers (ortho, meta, para) exist, supporting a symmetrical hexagonal ring (Kekule structure)."},{"id":"301S02","unit":1,"m":5,"q":"What is resonance energy? Explain the resonance energy of benzene from heat of hydrogenation.","hi":"Benzene का heat of hydrogenation expected से 36 kcal/mol कम = resonance energy","kp":[{"t":"Resonance energy: extra stability from delocalisation","k":["extra stabil","delocali","more stable"]},{"t":"Cyclohexene heat of hydrogenation 28.6 kcal/mol","k":["28.6","cyclohexene"]},{"t":"Expected for benzene 3 x 28.6 = 85.8 kcal/mol","k":["85.8","expected"]},{"t":"Observed for benzene 49.8 kcal/mol","k":["49.8","observed","actual"]},{"t":"Resonance energy about 36 kcal/mol (~150 kJ/mol)","k":["36","150 kj"]}],"a":"Resonance energy is the difference between the energy of the most stable contributing (Kekule) structure and the actual energy of the molecule; it is the extra stability due to delocalisation of electrons.\nHydrogenation of cyclohexene (one C=C) releases 28.6 kcal/mol. A Kekule benzene with three C=C should release 3 x 28.6 = 85.8 kcal/mol. Actually benzene releases only 49.8 kcal/mol. So benzene is about 36 kcal/mol (about 150 kJ/mol) more stable than expected. This 36 kcal/mol is the resonance energy of benzene, which explains its stability and its preference for substitution reactions."},{"id":"301S03","unit":1,"m":5,"q":"Describe the orbital picture of benzene.","hi":"6 sp2 carbon, ऊपर-नीचे delocalised π electron cloud, bond length 1.39 Å","kp":[{"t":"Each carbon sp2 hybridised, bond angle 120 degrees","k":["sp2","120"]},{"t":"Sigma bonds: C-C (sp2-sp2) and C-H (sp2-s), planar hexagon","k":["sigma","hexagon","planar"]},{"t":"Unhybridised p orbital on each carbon, perpendicular to plane","k":["unhybridi","p orbital","perpendicular"]},{"t":"Sideways overlap gives delocalised pi cloud above and below ring","k":["above and below","pi cloud","delocali","ring of electron"]},{"t":"All C-C bonds equal, 1.39 A","k":["1.39","equal"]}],"a":"In benzene each carbon is sp2 hybridised. The three sp2 orbitals form sigma bonds: two with neighbouring carbons (sp2-sp2) and one with hydrogen (sp2-1s). All 12 atoms lie in one plane with bond angles of 120 degrees, forming a regular hexagon.\nEach carbon has one unhybridised 2p orbital perpendicular to the ring plane. These six p orbitals overlap sideways with both neighbours, so the six pi electrons are delocalised as two doughnut-shaped electron clouds, one above and one below the ring.\nDue to delocalisation all C-C bonds are equal (1.39 A), between C-C single (1.54 A) and C=C double (1.34 A) bonds."},{"id":"301S04","unit":1,"m":5,"q":"Write the limitations of Friedel-Crafts alkylation. How can they be overcome?","hi":"Polyalkylation, carbocation rearrangement, deactivated ring पर reaction नहीं","kp":[{"t":"Polyalkylation (alkylbenzene more reactive)","k":["polyalkyl","poly alkyl","dialkyl"]},{"t":"Carbocation rearrangement (n-propyl chloride gives isopropylbenzene)","k":["rearrang","isopropyl","cumene"]},{"t":"Fails with strongly deactivated rings like nitrobenzene","k":["nitrobenzene","deactivat"]},{"t":"Fails with NH2/NHR rings: complex with AlCl3","k":["nh2","aniline","amino","complex"]},{"t":"Aryl and vinyl halides cannot be used","k":["aryl halide","vinyl","chlorobenzene"]},{"t":"Overcome by acylation followed by Clemmensen reduction","k":["clemmensen","acylation"]}],"a":"1. Polyalkylation: the alkyl group activates the ring, so the product is more reactive than benzene and di- and tri-alkylbenzenes are formed. Excess benzene is used to reduce this.\n2. Rearrangement: the carbocation can rearrange to a more stable one; n-propyl chloride gives mainly isopropylbenzene (cumene).\n3. Strongly deactivated rings (nitrobenzene, benzonitrile, benzoic acid) do not react; nitrobenzene is even used as a solvent.\n4. Rings carrying -NH2, -NHR or -NR2 fail because the amine forms a complex with AlCl3.\n5. Aryl and vinyl halides cannot be used as they do not form carbocations.\n6. The reaction is reversible, so isomerisation and dealkylation may occur. Most of these limitations are overcome by doing Friedel-Crafts acylation followed by Clemmensen reduction."},{"id":"301S05","unit":1,"m":5,"q":"Why is -NO2 group a meta director while -OH group is an ortho/para director?","hi":"OH lone pair से o/p पर charge stabilise; NO2 o/p intermediate को destabilise करता है","kp":[{"t":"-OH donates lone pair by +R/+M effect","k":["lone pair","+r","+m","resonance"]},{"t":"Extra resonance structure stabilises o/p arenium ion (oxonium)","k":["oxonium","extra","stabili"]},{"t":"-NO2 withdraws electrons (-R, -I)","k":["-r","-i","withdraw"]},{"t":"o/p attack puts + charge next to NO2 (very unstable)","k":["adjacent","next to","positive charge","destabili"]},{"t":"Meta attack is least destabilised","k":["meta","least"]}],"a":"Orientation depends on the stability of the arenium ion (sigma complex) formed in each attack.\n-OH: oxygen has lone pairs that it donates to the ring (+R effect). In ortho and para attack, one resonance form puts the positive charge on the carbon bearing -OH; the oxygen lone pair then forms an extra, very stable oxonium structure (every atom has an octet). So o/p intermediates are more stable and -OH is an activating o/p director.\n-NO2: strongly electron-withdrawing (-R and -I). In ortho/para attack one resonance form places + charge on the carbon carrying the positive nitrogen, which is highly unstable. Meta attack avoids this, so meta is the least destabilised path."},{"id":"301S06","unit":1,"m":2,"q":"State Huckel's rule.","hi":"(4n+2) π electron वाला planar cyclic conjugated ring aromatic","kp":[{"t":"Cyclic, planar, fully conjugated ring","k":["planar","cyclic","conjugat"]},{"t":"(4n+2) pi electrons, n = 0,1,2...","k":["4n+2","4n + 2","4n 2"]}],"a":"Huckel's rule: a monocyclic, planar compound in which every ring atom has a p orbital (complete conjugation) is aromatic if it contains (4n+2) pi electrons, where n = 0, 1, 2, 3... (2, 6, 10, 14 pi electrons). Example: benzene has 6 pi electrons (n = 1)."},{"id":"301S07","unit":1,"m":2,"q":"Why is sulphonation of benzene reversible? Give one application of this.","hi":"Dilute acid/steam से SO3H हट जाता है; blocking group की तरह use","kp":[{"t":"Heating with dilute acid / superheated steam removes SO3H","k":["dilute acid","steam","desulphon","desulfon"]},{"t":"Energy of forward and reverse steps similar (equilibrium)","k":["equilibri","reversib"]},{"t":"Used as temporary blocking group","k":["blocking","protect"]}],"a":"In sulphonation the energy barriers for forward and backward reactions are nearly equal, so an equilibrium exists. When benzenesulphonic acid is heated with dilute H2SO4 or superheated steam, the SO3H group is lost and benzene is regenerated (desulphonation). Application: SO3H is used as a temporary blocking group to direct another group to a desired position, then removed."},{"id":"301S08","unit":1,"m":2,"q":"Why does Friedel-Crafts acylation not give polysubstituted products, unlike alkylation?","hi":"Acyl group ring को deactivate करता है, alkyl group activate करता है","kp":[{"t":"Acyl (C=O) group is electron-withdrawing, deactivates ring","k":["deactivat","electron withdraw","withdraw"]},{"t":"Alkyl group activates ring, so polyalkylation","k":["activat","polyalkyl"]},{"t":"Ketone complexes with AlCl3, further reducing reactivity","k":["complex","alcl3"]}],"a":"In acylation the product is an aryl ketone. The -COR group is electron-withdrawing and deactivates the ring; the ketone also forms a complex with AlCl3. So the product is less reactive than benzene and a second acyl group does not enter. In alkylation the alkyl group is electron-donating, activates the ring, and the product reacts faster, giving polyalkylation."},{"id":"301S09","unit":1,"m":2,"q":"Why does n-propyl chloride give isopropylbenzene with benzene and AlCl3?","hi":"Primary carbocation 1,2-hydride shift से secondary बन जाता है","kp":[{"t":"Primary n-propyl carbocation formed first","k":["primary","n propyl","1 carbocation"]},{"t":"1,2-hydride shift (rearrangement)","k":["hydride shift","1 2 h","rearrang"]},{"t":"More stable secondary (isopropyl) carbocation gives cumene","k":["secondary","isopropyl","cumene"]}],"a":"With AlCl3, n-propyl chloride gives a primary n-propyl carbocation (or a polarised complex). This rearranges by a 1,2-hydride shift to the more stable secondary isopropyl carbocation, CH3-CH+-CH3. This attacks benzene, so the main product is isopropylbenzene (cumene), not n-propylbenzene."},{"id":"301S10","unit":1,"m":2,"q":"Halogens are deactivating but ortho-para directing. Explain.","hi":"-I effect से deactivation, +R (lone pair) से o/p orientation","kp":[{"t":"Strong -I effect withdraws electrons: deactivates ring","k":["-i","inductive"]},{"t":"+R effect: lone pair donation stabilises o/p arenium ion","k":["+r","lone pair","resonance","+m"]},{"t":"Inductive dominates reactivity, resonance controls orientation","k":["dominat","controls orientation","orientation"]}],"a":"Halogens are highly electronegative and withdraw electrons by the -I effect, so they make the ring less reactive than benzene (deactivating). But they also have lone pairs which they donate by the +R effect; in ortho and para attack an extra halonium resonance structure stabilises the arenium ion. Thus the -I effect controls reactivity, while the +R effect controls orientation (ortho/para)."},{"id":"301S11","unit":1,"m":2,"q":"Give the structure and uses of DDT.","hi":"DDT: p,p'-dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane; mosquito मारने का insecticide","kp":[{"t":"p,p'-Dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane","k":["dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane","dichloro diphenyl","bis 4 chlorophenyl"]},{"t":"Structure: (Cl-C6H4)2CH-CCl3","k":["ccl3","chlorophenyl"]},{"t":"Use: insecticide, mosquito/malaria control","k":["insecticid","mosquito","malaria"]}],"a":"DDT is p,p'-dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane, (Cl-C6H4)2CH-CCl3: a CCl3 group attached to a carbon carrying two p-chlorophenyl rings. It is made by condensing chlorobenzene with chloral in conc. H2SO4. Use: contact insecticide against mosquitoes (malaria) and lice. Being non-biodegradable it accumulates in fat, so its use is restricted."},{"id":"301S12","unit":1,"m":2,"q":"What is gammexane? Give its use.","hi":"BHC का gamma isomer = gammexane/lindane; scabies, lice","kp":[{"t":"Gamma isomer of benzene hexachloride (hexachlorocyclohexane)","k":["gamma","hexachlorocyclohexane","bhc","benzene hexachloride"]},{"t":"Also called lindane","k":["lindane","gammexane"]},{"t":"Use: insecticide; scabies and head lice","k":["scabies","lice","insecticid","pediculosis"]}],"a":"Gammexane is the gamma isomer of benzene hexachloride (BHC), i.e. 1,2,3,4,5,6-hexachlorocyclohexane (C6H6Cl6), also called lindane. BHC is made by adding three molecules of Cl2 to benzene in UV light. Gammexane is the most active isomer: it is used as an insecticide and as lindane lotion for scabies and head lice."},{"id":"301S13","unit":1,"m":2,"q":"Give the chemical name and uses of saccharin.","hi":"Saccharin = o-sulphobenzimide; sugar-free sweetener for diabetics","kp":[{"t":"o-Sulphobenzimide (benzoic sulphimide)","k":["sulphobenzimide","sulfobenzimide","sulphimide","benzisothiazol"]},{"t":"Artificial non-nutritive sweetener, hundreds of times sweeter than sugar","k":["sweeten","sweeter","non nutritive"]},{"t":"Used by diabetics / obese; sodium salt in syrups, tablets","k":["diabet","obes","sodium saccharin"]}],"a":"Saccharin is o-sulphobenzimide (1,2-benzisothiazol-3-one 1,1-dioxide), prepared from toluene. It is a non-nutritive artificial sweetener, several hundred times sweeter than sucrose, and passes out of the body unchanged (no calories). It is used by diabetic and obese patients and as sodium saccharin in syrups, tablets and toothpastes."},{"id":"301S14","unit":1,"m":2,"q":"Give the structure and uses of chloramine-T.","hi":"Chloramine-T: CH3C6H4SO2N(Cl)Na; antiseptic, water disinfectant","kp":[{"t":"Sodium N-chloro-p-toluenesulphonamide","k":["n chloro","toluenesulphonamide","toluenesulfonamide","so2n cl"]},{"t":"Releases active chlorine (hypochlorous acid)","k":["active chlorine","hypochlorous","chlorine"]},{"t":"Antiseptic and disinfectant (wounds, water)","k":["antiseptic","disinfect"]}],"a":"Chloramine-T is the sodium salt of N-chloro-p-toluenesulphonamide, CH3-C6H4-SO2-N(Cl)Na. In water it slowly liberates active chlorine as hypochlorous acid. Uses: antiseptic for wounds and mucous membranes, disinfectant for utensils and drinking water, and an oxidising reagent in the laboratory."},{"id":"301S15","unit":2,"m":5,"q":"Write a note on Schotten-Baumann reaction with examples and applications.","hi":"Phenol/amine + benzoyl chloride + aq NaOH → benzoyl derivative (ester/amide)","kp":[{"t":"Acylation/benzoylation with benzoyl chloride (C6H5COCl)","k":["benzoyl chloride","benzoylat","acylat","c6h5cocl"]},{"t":"In presence of aqueous NaOH (about 10%)","k":["naoh","alkali","aqueous sodium hydroxide"]},{"t":"Base neutralises HCl and shifts equilibrium","k":["neutrali","hcl"]},{"t":"Phenol gives phenyl benzoate","k":["phenyl benzoate","phenyl ester"]},{"t":"Aniline gives benzanilide","k":["benzanilide","n phenylbenzamide"]},{"t":"Use: solid derivatives for identification, protection of OH/NH2","k":["identif","derivative","protect"]}],"a":"Schotten-Baumann reaction is the acylation (usually benzoylation) of phenols, alcohols or primary/secondary amines with an acid chloride such as benzoyl chloride in the presence of aqueous sodium hydroxide (about 10%).\nC6H5OH + C6H5COCl + NaOH -> C6H5OCOC6H5 (phenyl benzoate) + NaCl + H2O\nC6H5NH2 + C6H5COCl + NaOH -> C6H5NHCOC6H5 (benzanilide) + NaCl + H2O\nRole of NaOH: converts phenol to the more nucleophilic phenoxide ion and neutralises the HCl formed, so the equilibrium moves forward and the amine is not lost as its salt.\nApplications: preparing crystalline benzoyl derivatives (sharp melting points) to identify phenols and amines; protection of -OH and -NH2 groups; synthesis of esters and amides."},{"id":"301S16","unit":2,"m":5,"q":"Explain Williamson ether synthesis and Claisen rearrangement with reference to phenols.","hi":"Phenoxide + alkyl halide → aryl ether; allyl phenyl ether गरम करने पर o-allylphenol","kp":[{"t":"Williamson: sodium phenoxide + alkyl halide gives aryl alkyl ether","k":["phenoxide","williamson"]},{"t":"Example: anisole from sodium phenoxide + CH3I","k":["anisole","methyl iodide","ch3i"]},{"t":"SN2 mechanism; aryl halide + alkoxide does not work","k":["sn2","aryl halide"]},{"t":"Claisen: allyl phenyl ether heated to about 200 C","k":["allyl phenyl ether","claisen"]},{"t":"Gives o-allylphenol via [3,3] sigmatropic cyclic transition state","k":["o allylphenol","allylphenol","sigmatropic","3 3","cyclic"]},{"t":"If both ortho blocked, allyl goes to para","k":["para","blocked"]}],"a":"Williamson ether synthesis: an alkoxide or phenoxide ion reacts with an alkyl halide by SN2 to give an ether. For aryl ethers, sodium phenoxide is treated with the alkyl halide:\nC6H5O-Na+ + CH3I -> C6H5OCH3 (anisole) + NaI.\nThe reverse combination (aryl halide + sodium alkoxide) fails because aryl halides do not undergo SN2.\nClaisen rearrangement: when allyl phenyl ether is heated to about 200 C, the allyl group migrates from oxygen to the ortho carbon, giving o-allylphenol. It is an intramolecular [3,3]-sigmatropic rearrangement through a cyclic six-membered transition state, followed by tautomerisation (keto to enol). If both ortho positions are blocked, the allyl group ends up at the para position."},{"id":"301S17","unit":2,"m":5,"q":"Why are aromatic amines less basic than aliphatic amines and ammonia? Explain the effect of substituents on the basicity of aniline.","hi":"Aniline का lone pair ring में delocalise; EWG basicity घटाते, EDG बढ़ाते","kp":[{"t":"Lone pair on N delocalised into benzene ring (resonance)","k":["lone pair","delocali","resonance"]},{"t":"Anilinium ion has no resonance stabilisation; aniline is stabilised","k":["anilinium","stabili"]},{"t":"sp2 carbon of ring is electron-withdrawing (-I)","k":["sp2","-i","inductive"]},{"t":"Aliphatic amines more basic due to +I of alkyl groups","k":["+i","alkyl","methylamine"]},{"t":"EDG (-CH3, -OCH3) at para increase basicity (p-toluidine)","k":["toluidine","anisidine","electron donat"]},{"t":"EWG (-NO2, -Cl) decrease basicity (p-nitroaniline); ortho effect","k":["nitroaniline","withdraw","ortho effect"]}],"a":"Basicity depends on the availability of the nitrogen lone pair to accept a proton.\nIn aniline the lone pair is delocalised into the benzene ring by resonance, so it is less available; aniline is stabilised by resonance but the anilinium ion (C6H5NH3+) is not, so protonation is unfavourable. The sp2 ring carbon also withdraws electrons (-I). In aliphatic amines the alkyl groups push electrons (+I) toward N. Order: CH3NH2 (pKb 3.4) > NH3 (4.75) > C6H5NH2 (9.4).\nSubstituent effects: electron-donating groups (-CH3, -OCH3) at the para position increase basicity (p-toluidine, p-anisidine > aniline). Electron-withdrawing groups (-NO2, -CN, -Cl) decrease basicity; p-nitroaniline is a very weak base. Almost all ortho-substituted anilines are weaker bases than aniline (ortho effect)."},{"id":"301S18","unit":2,"m":5,"q":"Explain Kolbe-Schmitt reaction and Reimer-Tiemann reaction with mechanism.","hi":"Kolbe: phenoxide + CO2 → salicylic acid; Reimer-Tiemann: CHCl3/NaOH → salicylaldehyde","kp":[{"t":"Kolbe: sodium phenoxide + CO2 under pressure, about 125 C","k":["co2","carbon dioxide","kolbe"]},{"t":"Kolbe product: salicylic acid (o-hydroxybenzoic acid)","k":["salicylic acid","hydroxybenzoic"]},{"t":"Reimer-Tiemann: phenol + CHCl3 + aqueous NaOH","k":["chcl3","chloroform","reimer"]},{"t":"Electrophile dichlorocarbene :CCl2","k":["dichlorocarbene","carbene","ccl2"]},{"t":"Product salicylaldehyde (o-hydroxybenzaldehyde)","k":["salicylaldehyde","hydroxybenzaldehyde"]},{"t":"With CCl4 instead gives salicylic acid","k":["ccl4","carbon tetrachloride"]}],"a":"Kolbe-Schmitt reaction: sodium phenoxide is heated with CO2 at about 125 C under pressure (4-7 atm); acidification gives salicylic acid (o-hydroxybenzoic acid). Mechanism: the phenoxide ion, a strongly activated ring, attacks the weak electrophile CO2 at the ortho position; tautomerisation gives sodium salicylate. Salicylic acid is used to make aspirin and methyl salicylate.\nReimer-Tiemann reaction: phenol is heated (about 60-70 C) with chloroform and aqueous NaOH, then acidified, giving salicylaldehyde (o-hydroxybenzaldehyde). Mechanism: CHCl3 + OH- -> :CCl3- -> :CCl2 (dichlorocarbene, electrophile) + Cl-; the phenoxide ion attacks :CCl2 at the ortho position; the -CHCl2 group formed is hydrolysed to -CHO. With CCl4 in place of CHCl3, salicylic acid is formed."},{"id":"301S19","unit":2,"m":5,"q":"Give the structure and uses of phenol, cresols, resorcinol and naphthols.","hi":"Phenol antiseptic, cresol Lysol, resorcinol acne/keratolytic, naphthol dyes","kp":[{"t":"Phenol C6H5OH (carbolic acid): antiseptic, disinfectant","k":["carbolic","c6h5oh","antiseptic"]},{"t":"Cresols: o-, m-, p-methylphenols (CH3C6H4OH)","k":["methylphenol","ch3c6h4oh","cresol"]},{"t":"Cresol uses: disinfectant (Lysol = cresol + soap), preservative","k":["lysol","soap","disinfect"]},{"t":"Resorcinol: 1,3-dihydroxybenzene, keratolytic in acne/eczema","k":["1 3 dihydroxy","dihydroxybenzene","keratolytic","acne"]},{"t":"Naphthols: alpha (1-) and beta (2-) hydroxynaphthalene, C10H7OH","k":["1 naphthol","2 naphthol","c10h7oh","hydroxynaphthalene"]},{"t":"Naphthol uses: azo dyes; alpha-naphthol in Molisch test","k":["azo","dye","molisch"]}],"a":"Phenol (carbolic acid), C6H5OH, a hydroxyl group on benzene: antiseptic and disinfectant, standard in phenol coefficient test, used to make bakelite, salicylic acid/aspirin and picric acid.\nCresols, CH3-C6H4-OH (o-, m- and p-methylphenol): stronger germicides than phenol; cresol with soap solution (Lysol) is a disinfectant; m-cresol is a preservative in injections such as insulin.\nResorcinol, 1,3-dihydroxybenzene (m-dihydroxybenzene): keratolytic and antiseptic used in acne, eczema, psoriasis and seborrhoeic dermatitis; used to make fluorescein.\nNaphthols, C10H7OH: alpha-naphthol (1-naphthol) and beta-naphthol (2-naphthol). Used to make azo dyes (coupling with diazonium salts gives orange-red dyes); alpha-naphthol is the reagent of Molisch's test for carbohydrates; beta-naphthol has antiseptic and antifungal action."},{"id":"301S20","unit":2,"m":2,"q":"Why is phenol more acidic than ethanol?","hi":"Phenoxide ion resonance से stable, ethoxide नहीं","kp":[{"t":"Phenoxide ion stabilised by resonance (charge delocalised into ring)","k":["phenoxide","resonance","delocali"]},{"t":"Ethoxide not stabilised; +I of ethyl destabilises","k":["ethoxide","+i","not stabil"]},{"t":"pKa phenol about 10 vs ethanol about 16","k":["pka 10","10","16"]}],"a":"After losing H+, phenol forms the phenoxide ion, whose negative charge is delocalised over the ortho and para carbons of the ring by resonance, so it is stabilised. The ethoxide ion has no resonance and its charge is intensified by the +I effect of the ethyl group. Hence phenol (pKa about 10) is more acidic than ethanol (pKa about 16)."},{"id":"301S21","unit":2,"m":2,"q":"Why is p-nitrophenol more acidic than phenol, whereas p-cresol is less acidic?","hi":"NO2 (EWG) phenoxide को stable करता है; CH3 (EDG) destabilise","kp":[{"t":"-NO2 withdraws electrons (-R, -I), stabilises phenoxide","k":["no2","withdraw","stabili"]},{"t":"Negative charge delocalised onto nitro oxygen at para","k":["nitro oxygen","delocali","para"]},{"t":"-CH3 donates electrons (+I, hyperconjugation), destabilises phenoxide","k":["ch3","+i","hyperconjug","destabili"]}],"a":"The nitro group is strongly electron-withdrawing (-R and -I); in p-nitrophenoxide the negative charge is delocalised onto the oxygens of the NO2 group, giving extra stability, so p-nitrophenol (pKa about 7.1) is more acidic than phenol (about 10). The methyl group in p-cresol donates electrons (+I and hyperconjugation), intensifying the negative charge on the phenoxide, so p-cresol is less acidic."},{"id":"301S22","unit":2,"m":2,"q":"Why does phenol not liberate CO2 from sodium bicarbonate solution while benzoic acid does?","hi":"Phenol (pKa 10) carbonic acid से weak है; benzoic acid (pKa 4.2) strong","kp":[{"t":"Phenol weaker acid than carbonic acid","k":["carbonic acid","weaker"]},{"t":"Benzoic acid (pKa 4.2) stronger than carbonic acid","k":["4.2","stronger"]},{"t":"Carboxylate has two equivalent resonance forms on oxygens","k":["carboxylate","equivalent","two oxygen"]}],"a":"Only an acid stronger than carbonic acid (pKa about 6.4) can liberate CO2 from NaHCO3. Phenol (pKa about 10) is weaker than carbonic acid, so no effervescence occurs. Benzoic acid (pKa 4.2) is stronger because its carboxylate ion is stabilised by two equivalent resonance structures with the charge on two oxygen atoms. This test distinguishes phenols from carboxylic acids."},{"id":"301S23","unit":2,"m":2,"q":"Write the ferric chloride test and bromine water test for phenol.","hi":"FeCl3 से violet colour; bromine water से white ppt (2,4,6-tribromophenol)","kp":[{"t":"Neutral FeCl3 gives violet colour","k":["violet","purple","fecl3","ferric chloride"]},{"t":"Bromine water gives white precipitate","k":["white precipitate","white ppt","bromine water"]},{"t":"Product 2,4,6-tribromophenol","k":["tribromophenol","2 4 6"]}],"a":"Ferric chloride test: an aqueous solution of phenol gives a violet colour with neutral FeCl3 solution due to formation of a coloured iron-phenoxide complex. Bromine water test: phenol decolourises bromine water and gives a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenol, because the -OH group strongly activates the ortho and para positions."},{"id":"301S24","unit":2,"m":2,"q":"What is diazotisation? Why is it carried out at 0-5 C?","hi":"ArNH2 + NaNO2 + HCl (0-5 °C) → ArN2+Cl-; गरम होने पर decompose","kp":[{"t":"Primary aromatic amine + NaNO2 + HCl gives diazonium salt","k":["nano2","nitrous acid","diazonium"]},{"t":"Example: aniline to benzenediazonium chloride","k":["benzenediazonium","c6h5n2"]},{"t":"0-5 C because diazonium salts decompose (to phenol + N2) at higher temperature","k":["0 5","decompos","unstable","ice"]}],"a":"Diazotisation is the conversion of a primary aromatic amine into a diazonium salt by treating it with nitrous acid (NaNO2 + HCl) at 0-5 C:\nC6H5NH2 + NaNO2 + 2HCl -> C6H5N2+Cl- + NaCl + 2H2O.\nThe low temperature is needed because diazonium salts are unstable; above about 5 C they decompose, reacting with water to give phenol and nitrogen gas."},{"id":"301S25","unit":2,"m":2,"q":"What is Sandmeyer reaction? How does Gattermann reaction differ from it?","hi":"Sandmeyer: CuCl/CuBr/CuCN; Gattermann: Cu powder + HX","kp":[{"t":"Diazonium salt + CuCl/HCl, CuBr/HBr or CuCN gives ArCl, ArBr, ArCN","k":["cucl","cubr","cucn","cuprous"]},{"t":"Example: chlorobenzene / benzonitrile formation","k":["chlorobenzene","bromobenzene","benzonitrile"]},{"t":"Gattermann uses copper powder with HX instead of cuprous salt","k":["copper powder","cu powder","gattermann"]}],"a":"Sandmeyer reaction: an aryl diazonium salt is replaced by Cl, Br or CN when warmed with the corresponding cuprous salt: C6H5N2+Cl- + CuCl/HCl -> C6H5Cl + N2; with CuBr/HBr gives bromobenzene; with CuCN/KCN gives benzonitrile.\nGattermann reaction: the same replacement is done using copper powder with HCl or HBr instead of cuprous salts; yields are usually lower."},{"id":"301S26","unit":2,"m":2,"q":"What is a coupling reaction of diazonium salts? Give one example.","hi":"ArN2+ + phenol/aniline → azo dye (-N=N-), para position पर","kp":[{"t":"Diazonium ion (weak electrophile) attacks activated ring (phenol/amine)","k":["phenol","aniline","electrophile"]},{"t":"Forms azo compound -N=N- (coloured dye)","k":["azo","n=n","dye"]},{"t":"Example: p-hydroxyazobenzene (orange) in mildly alkaline medium","k":["hydroxyazobenzene","aminoazobenzene","alkaline","naphthol"]}],"a":"In a coupling reaction the aryl diazonium ion acts as a weak electrophile and attacks a strongly activated aromatic ring (phenol or aromatic amine), usually at the para position, forming an azo compound (Ar-N=N-Ar') which is coloured. Example: benzenediazonium chloride + phenol in mildly alkaline solution -> p-hydroxyazobenzene (orange dye). With beta-naphthol an orange-red dye is formed (test for primary aromatic amines)."},{"id":"301S27","unit":2,"m":2,"q":"Why are ortho-substituted benzoic acids generally stronger acids than benzoic acid?","hi":"Ortho effect: steric push से COOH ring से twist, resonance कम","kp":[{"t":"Ortho effect (steric + electronic)","k":["ortho effect","ortho substitu"]},{"t":"COOH twisted out of plane of ring, less resonance with ring","k":["twist","out of plane","steric"]},{"t":"Example: o-toluic acid / salicylic acid stronger than benzoic acid","k":["toluic","salicylic","hydrogen bond"]}],"a":"This is the ortho effect. An ortho substituent, whatever its electronic nature, sterically twists the -COOH group out of the plane of the ring. This reduces the ring-carboxyl resonance that normally lowers acidity, so the acid becomes stronger. Example: o-toluic acid is stronger than benzoic acid; salicylic acid is much stronger due to intramolecular H-bonding in its anion."},{"id":"301S28","unit":2,"m":2,"q":"Why is aniline acetylated before nitration?","hi":"Direct nitration में oxidation + anilinium (meta); acetanilide से p-nitroaniline","kp":[{"t":"Direct nitration oxidises aniline (tarry products)","k":["oxidi","tar"]},{"t":"In acid, anilinium ion forms, giving meta product","k":["anilinium","meta"]},{"t":"Acetanilide is moderately activating, gives mainly para; hydrolysis gives p-nitroaniline","k":["acetanilide","p nitroaniline","protect","hydroly"]}],"a":"Nitric acid oxidises the strongly activated aniline ring, giving tarry products, and in the strongly acidic mixture aniline is protonated to the anilinium ion (-NH3+, meta directing), so much m-nitroaniline forms. Acetylation gives acetanilide: the -NHCOCH3 group is less activating, protects from oxidation and directs mainly para. Nitration then hydrolysis gives p-nitroaniline."},{"id":"301S29","unit":3,"m":5,"q":"What is rancidity? Explain its types, causes and prevention.","hi":"Rancidity: तेल में बदबू; hydrolytic, oxidative, ketonic; antioxidant से रोकें","kp":[{"t":"Rancidity: unpleasant odour/taste on storage","k":["unpleasant","odour","odor","bad smell"]},{"t":"Hydrolytic: lipase/moisture frees fatty acids (butyric acid in butter)","k":["hydrolytic","lipase","butyric"]},{"t":"Oxidative: O2 attacks double bonds, peroxides, aldehydes","k":["oxidative","peroxide","autoxid","aldehyde"]},{"t":"Ketonic/microbial: moulds give methyl ketones","k":["ketonic","microb","mould","mold","methyl ketone"]},{"t":"Catalysed by light, heat, moisture, metals (Cu, Fe)","k":["light","heat","metal","copper"]},{"t":"Prevention: antioxidants (BHA, BHT, tocopherol), cool dark airtight","k":["antioxidant","bha","bht","tocopherol"]}],"a":"Rancidity is the development of an unpleasant smell and taste in fats and oils on storage.\n1. Hydrolytic rancidity: moisture and lipase enzymes (from microbes) hydrolyse triglycerides into glycerol and free fatty acids; short-chain acids such as butyric acid give butter its rancid smell.\n2. Oxidative rancidity: atmospheric oxygen attacks the double bonds of unsaturated fatty acids by a free-radical chain (autoxidation), forming hydroperoxides that break down into aldehydes and ketones. Light, heat and traces of metals (Cu, Fe) catalyse it.\n3. Ketonic (microbial) rancidity: moulds oxidise fatty acids to methyl ketones (coconut oil, butter).\nPrevention: store in cool, dark, dry, well-filled airtight containers; add antioxidants (BHA, BHT, propyl gallate, tocopherol) and chelating agents (citric acid, EDTA); hydrogenation."},{"id":"301S30","unit":3,"m":5,"q":"Write a note on drying oils.","hi":"Drying oil (linseed): highly unsaturated, हवा में O2 लेकर hard film बनाता है","kp":[{"t":"Oils rich in polyunsaturated acids (linolenic, linoleic)","k":["linolenic","linoleic","polyunsaturat"]},{"t":"Absorb oxygen, polymerise to hard elastic film","k":["oxygen","polymeri","film","cross link"]},{"t":"Examples: linseed oil, tung oil","k":["linseed","tung"]},{"t":"Classification by iodine value: drying >130, semi-drying 100-130, non-drying <100","k":["130","iodine value","semi drying","non drying"]},{"t":"Uses: paints, varnishes, printing inks, linoleum","k":["paint","varnish","ink","linoleum"]}],"a":"Drying oils are vegetable oils that, when spread as a thin layer and exposed to air, absorb oxygen and harden into a tough, elastic, transparent film.\nThey are rich in polyunsaturated fatty acids (linolenic and linoleic acid). Drying occurs by autoxidation at the double bonds followed by polymerisation and cross-linking. Metal salts of cobalt, manganese or lead (driers) speed it up.\nClassification by iodine value: drying oils, above 130 (linseed, tung); semi-drying oils, 100-130 (soybean, cottonseed, sesame); non-drying oils, below 100 (olive, groundnut, castor).\nUses: paints, varnishes, printing inks, linoleum and oil cloth."},{"id":"301S31","unit":3,"m":5,"q":"Explain hydrogenation (hardening) of oils. Give its significance.","hi":"Oil + H2 (Ni catalyst) → solid fat (vanaspati); rancidity कम","kp":[{"t":"Addition of H2 to C=C double bonds of unsaturated glycerides","k":["double bond","unsaturat","add hydrogen","h2"]},{"t":"Finely divided nickel catalyst, heat and pressure","k":["nickel","ni"]},{"t":"Liquid oil becomes semi-solid fat (vanaspati, margarine)","k":["vanaspati","margarine","solid"]},{"t":"Raises melting point, lowers iodine value, more stable to rancidity","k":["melting point","iodine value","rancid"]},{"t":"Partial hydrogenation forms trans fats","k":["trans","trans fat","trans isomer"]}],"a":"Hydrogenation (hardening) is the addition of hydrogen to the carbon-carbon double bonds of the unsaturated fatty acid chains of an oil. Purified oil is heated and stirred with hydrogen under pressure in the presence of finely divided nickel catalyst. Oleic acid residues become stearic acid residues.\nSignificance: liquid oils are converted into semi-solid fats (vanaspati ghee, margarine, shortenings); the melting point rises and the iodine value falls; the product is more resistant to oxidative rancidity and has longer shelf life; hardened oils are used for soaps and as ointment and suppository bases.\nDrawback: partial hydrogenation isomerises some cis double bonds to trans, forming trans fats that are harmful to heart health."},{"id":"301S32","unit":3,"m":5,"q":"Define Reichert-Meissl value. Explain the principle of its determination and significance.","hi":"RM value: 5 g fat के volatile water-soluble fatty acids, ml 0.1 N alkali; ghee test","kp":[{"t":"ml of 0.1 N alkali for volatile water-soluble fatty acids","k":["0.1 n","volatile","water soluble"]},{"t":"From 5 g of fat","k":["5 g","5 gram"]},{"t":"Saponify, acidify, steam distil, titrate distillate","k":["saponif","distil","acidif"]},{"t":"Measures short-chain acids (butyric, caproic)","k":["butyric","caproic","short chain"]},{"t":"Significance: butter/ghee high RM, detects adulteration","k":["butter","ghee","adulterat"]}],"a":"Reichert-Meissl (RM) value is the number of ml of 0.1 N aqueous alkali required to neutralise the volatile, water-soluble fatty acids distilled from 5 g of fat under specified conditions.\nPrinciple: 5 g of fat is saponified (glycerol-NaOH), the soap is dissolved in water and acidified with dilute H2SO4 to free the fatty acids. The mixture is distilled under fixed conditions; the volatile water-soluble acids (butyric, caproic) pass into the distillate, which is filtered and titrated with 0.1 N NaOH using phenolphthalein. A blank is run.\nSignificance: butter fat and ghee have a high RM value because they contain butyric acid, while most vegetable oils and animal body fats have very low values; so RM value detects adulteration of butter and ghee. Polenske value measures the volatile water-insoluble acids."},{"id":"301S33","unit":3,"m":2,"q":"Define acid value. Give its significance.","hi":"Acid value: 1 g fat के free fatty acid को neutralise करने में mg KOH","kp":[{"t":"mg of KOH to neutralise free fatty acids","k":["mg koh","mg of koh","free fatty acid"]},{"t":"In 1 g of fat/oil","k":["1 g","one gram"]},{"t":"Indicates rancidity / freshness / quality","k":["rancid","fresh","quality","hydroly"]}],"a":"Acid value is the number of mg of KOH required to neutralise the free fatty acids present in 1 g of fat or oil. Significance: fresh oils have a low acid value; it rises when fat is hydrolysed on storage, so a high acid value shows hydrolytic rancidity, poor storage or poor refining."},{"id":"301S34","unit":3,"m":2,"q":"Define saponification value. How is it related to the molecular weight of a fat?","hi":"SV: 1 g fat saponify करने में mg KOH; SV ∝ 1/molecular weight","kp":[{"t":"mg KOH to saponify 1 g of fat","k":["mg koh","saponify 1 g","1 g"]},{"t":"Inversely proportional to molecular weight / chain length","k":["inversely","molecular weight","chain length"]},{"t":"Short-chain fats (coconut oil) have high SV","k":["coconut","short chain","high"]}],"a":"Saponification value is the number of mg of KOH required to completely saponify 1 g of fat or oil (free acids plus esters). It is inversely proportional to the mean molecular weight of the fat: fats with short-chain fatty acids (coconut oil, butter) have high SV, while long-chain fats have low SV. Mean molecular weight of a triglyceride = 3 x 56100 / SV."},{"id":"301S35","unit":3,"m":2,"q":"What is ester value? How is it calculated?","hi":"Ester value = saponification value - acid value","kp":[{"t":"mg KOH to saponify esters in 1 g","k":["ester","mg koh"]},{"t":"Ester value = SV - AV","k":["sv av","sv minus av","minus","difference","ester value"]}],"a":"Ester value is the number of mg of KOH required to saponify the esters (glycerides) present in 1 g of fat or oil. It is calculated as: Ester value = Saponification value - Acid value. It indicates the amount of combined (esterified) fatty acids; a high ester value means most of the fatty acids are present as glycerides."},{"id":"301S36","unit":3,"m":2,"q":"Define iodine value. What is its significance?","hi":"Iodine value = 100 g oil में absorbed I2 (g); unsaturation का माप","kp":[{"t":"Grams of iodine absorbed by 100 g of fat","k":["grams of iodine","100 g","iodine absorbed"]},{"t":"Measures degree of unsaturation","k":["unsaturat","double bond"]},{"t":"Classifies drying/semi-drying/non-drying oils; detects adulteration","k":["drying","adulterat","classif"]}],"a":"Iodine value is the number of grams of iodine absorbed by 100 g of fat or oil. It measures the degree of unsaturation (number of double bonds). Significance: oils have higher IV than fats; it classifies oils as drying (above 130), semi-drying (100-130) and non-drying (below 100), and helps detect adulteration and check hydrogenation."},{"id":"301S37","unit":3,"m":2,"q":"Define acetyl value. What does it indicate?","hi":"Acetyl value: free -OH groups का माप; castor oil में high","kp":[{"t":"mg KOH to neutralise acetic acid from 1 g acetylated fat","k":["acetic acid","acetylated","mg koh"]},{"t":"Measures free hydroxyl groups","k":["hydroxyl","oh group"]},{"t":"Castor oil high (ricinoleic acid)","k":["castor","ricinoleic"]}],"a":"Acetyl value is the number of mg of KOH required to neutralise the acetic acid obtained when 1 g of acetylated fat or oil is saponified. It measures the free hydroxyl groups present (hydroxy fatty acids, mono- and diglycerides, free alcohols). Castor oil has a high acetyl value because of ricinoleic acid (a hydroxy fatty acid)."},{"id":"301S38","unit":3,"m":2,"q":"Differentiate between fats and oils.","hi":"Fat solid (saturated, animal), oil liquid (unsaturated, plant)","kp":[{"t":"Fats solid, oils liquid at room temperature","k":["solid","liquid","room temperature"]},{"t":"Fats have more saturated acids; oils more unsaturated","k":["saturated","unsaturated"]},{"t":"Oils have higher iodine value; fats mostly animal, oils mostly plant","k":["iodine value","animal","plant","vegetable"]}],"a":"Both are triglycerides (esters of glycerol with fatty acids).\nFats: solid or semi-solid at room temperature, contain mainly saturated fatty acids (palmitic, stearic), low iodine value, mostly of animal origin (e.g. lard, butter).\nOils: liquid at room temperature, contain mainly unsaturated fatty acids (oleic, linoleic), higher iodine value, mostly of plant origin (e.g. groundnut, olive oil); more prone to oxidative rancidity."},{"id":"301S39","unit":3,"m":2,"q":"What is saponification? Write the reaction.","hi":"Fat + NaOH → soap + glycerol","kp":[{"t":"Alkaline hydrolysis of fat with NaOH/KOH","k":["alkaline hydrolysis","naoh","koh"]},{"t":"Products: soap (sodium salts of fatty acids) + glycerol","k":["soap","glycerol","glycerin"]},{"t":"Sodium soaps hard, potassium soaps soft","k":["hard","soft","potassium"]}],"a":"Saponification is the alkaline hydrolysis of fats or oils with NaOH or KOH, giving glycerol and the sodium or potassium salts of fatty acids (soap).\nTriglyceride + 3NaOH -> glycerol + 3 RCOONa (soap).\nSodium soaps are hard, potassium soaps are soft. It is the basis of soap manufacture and of saponification value."},{"id":"301S40","unit":3,"m":2,"q":"Classify fatty acids with examples. What are essential fatty acids?","hi":"Saturated (palmitic), unsaturated (oleic, linoleic); EFA शरीर नहीं बनाता","kp":[{"t":"Saturated: palmitic (C16), stearic (C18)","k":["palmitic","stearic","saturated"]},{"t":"Unsaturated: oleic, linoleic, linolenic, arachidonic","k":["oleic","linoleic","linolenic","arachidonic"]},{"t":"Essential fatty acids not made by body, must be in diet (linoleic, linolenic)","k":["essential","diet","not synthesi"]}],"a":"Fatty acids are long-chain monocarboxylic acids. Saturated: lauric (C12), myristic (C14), palmitic (C16), stearic (C18). Unsaturated: monounsaturated oleic (C18, one C=C) and polyunsaturated linoleic (2 C=C), linolenic (3 C=C), arachidonic (C20, 4 C=C). Special: hydroxy acid ricinoleic (castor oil). Essential fatty acids are those the body cannot synthesise and must get from diet: linoleic and alpha-linolenic acid."},{"id":"301S41","unit":3,"m":2,"q":"Write any two reactions of oleic acid involving its double bond.","hi":"Oleic acid + H2 → stearic acid; + HNO2 → elaidic acid (trans)","kp":[{"t":"Hydrogenation (H2/Ni) gives stearic acid","k":["stearic","hydrogenat"]},{"t":"Addition of iodine/bromine (basis of iodine value)","k":["iodine","bromine","halogen"]},{"t":"Cold dilute KMnO4 gives 9,10-dihydroxystearic acid","k":["dihydroxystearic","kmno4"]},{"t":"Nitrous acid isomerises to elaidic acid (trans)","k":["elaidic","elaidin","nitrous"]}],"a":"1. Hydrogenation: oleic acid + H2 (Ni catalyst) -> stearic acid (saturated, solid).\n2. Halogenation: it adds I2 or Br2 across the double bond, the basis of iodine value.\n3. Oxidation: cold dilute alkaline KMnO4 gives 9,10-dihydroxystearic acid; hot KMnO4 cleaves the chain.\n4. Elaidinisation: nitrous acid converts cis-oleic acid into its trans isomer, elaidic acid."},{"id":"301S42","unit":4,"m":5,"q":"Write a note on Diels-Alder reaction with reference to anthracene.","hi":"Diene + dienophile → cyclohexene ring; anthracene 9,10 पर maleic anhydride जोड़ता है","kp":[{"t":"[4+2] cycloaddition of conjugated diene and dienophile","k":["4+2","4 2","cycloaddition","diene","dienophile"]},{"t":"Forms six-membered cyclohexene ring in one step (concerted)","k":["six membered","cyclohexene","concerted"]},{"t":"Diene must be s-cis; dienophile activated by EWG","k":["s cis","electron withdraw","ewg"]},{"t":"Stereospecific / endo rule","k":["stereospecific","endo","syn addition"]},{"t":"Anthracene reacts at 9,10 with maleic anhydride","k":["9 10","maleic anhydride","anthracene"]}],"a":"Diels-Alder reaction is a [4+2] cycloaddition in which a conjugated diene reacts with an alkene or alkyne (dienophile) to form a six-membered ring (cyclohexene) in a single concerted step, forming two new sigma bonds. Example: 1,3-butadiene + ethylene -> cyclohexene.\nFeatures: the diene must be in the s-cis conformation; electron-withdrawing groups on the dienophile (maleic anhydride, acrolein) speed it up; it is stereospecific (syn addition) and the endo product is preferred.\nAnthracene: the central ring acts as a diene; anthracene adds maleic anhydride across its 9,10 positions to give a bridged adduct, leaving two intact benzene rings. This shows the high reactivity of the 9,10 positions. Naphthalene and phenanthrene react far less readily."},{"id":"301S43","unit":4,"m":5,"q":"Explain electrophilic substitution in naphthalene. Why does it occur at the alpha position? Explain the effect of temperature on its sulphonation.","hi":"α-attack का intermediate ज्यादा stable (2 benzenoid forms); 80 °C α, 160 °C β","kp":[{"t":"Naphthalene more reactive than benzene; substitution mainly at alpha (C1)","k":["alpha","c1","1 position"]},{"t":"Alpha intermediate has more resonance forms keeping one benzene ring intact","k":["benzenoid","intact","more resonance","more stable"]},{"t":"Nitration gives 1-nitronaphthalene; bromination 1-bromonaphthalene","k":["1 nitronaphthalene","nitronaphthalene","bromonaphthalene"]},{"t":"Sulphonation at about 80 C gives 1-sulphonic acid (kinetic)","k":["80","kinetic","1 sulphonic","1 sulfonic"]},{"t":"At about 160 C gives 2-sulphonic acid (thermodynamic, less steric)","k":["160","thermodynamic","2 sulphonic","2 sulfonic","peri"]}],"a":"Naphthalene undergoes electrophilic substitution more readily than benzene, mainly at the alpha position (C1).\nReason: attack at C1 gives an arenium ion with two resonance structures that keep one benzene ring fully aromatic (benzenoid), whereas attack at C2 gives only one such structure. So the alpha intermediate is more stable and forms faster. Examples: nitration gives 1-nitronaphthalene; bromination gives 1-bromonaphthalene.\nSulphonation: at about 80 C naphthalene-1-sulphonic acid is formed (kinetic control, faster). At about 160 C naphthalene-2-sulphonic acid is formed (thermodynamic control) because the 1-isomer is destabilised by steric clash of SO3H with the peri hydrogen at C8, and sulphonation is reversible so the more stable 2-isomer accumulates."},{"id":"301S44","unit":4,"m":5,"q":"Give the preparation of triphenylmethane and the structure and uses of its important derivatives.","hi":"Benzene + CHCl3 (AlCl3) → triphenylmethane; dyes: crystal violet, malachite green","kp":[{"t":"Friedel-Crafts: benzene + chloroform with AlCl3","k":["chloroform","chcl3","alcl3"]},{"t":"Structure (C6H5)3CH; trityl cation/radical/anion stable","k":["c6h5 3ch","trityl","triphenylmethyl"]},{"t":"Oxidation gives triphenylcarbinol","k":["triphenylcarbinol","triphenylmethanol"]},{"t":"Dyes: crystal violet (gentian violet), malachite green, rosaniline","k":["crystal violet","gentian violet","malachite","rosaniline"]},{"t":"Phenolphthalein indicator; clotrimazole antifungal","k":["phenolphthalein","clotrimazole","antifungal"]}],"a":"Triphenylmethane, (C6H5)3CH, has three phenyl rings on one carbon.\nPreparation: Friedel-Crafts reaction of benzene with chloroform in presence of anhydrous AlCl3: 3C6H6 + CHCl3 -> (C6H5)3CH + 3HCl. (With CCl4, triphenylmethyl chloride is formed, which can be reduced.)\nReactions: oxidation gives triphenylcarbinol, (C6H5)3COH; the central C-H gives very stable triphenylmethyl (trityl) cation, radical and anion because the charge or odd electron is delocalised over three rings.\nImportant derivatives: triphenylmethane dyes - crystal violet (gentian violet, antiseptic and antifungal), malachite green, brilliant green (antiseptic), rosaniline (magenta, basic fuchsin, used in staining); phenolphthalein (acid-base indicator, earlier a laxative); clotrimazole (antifungal) contains a trityl group."},{"id":"301S45","unit":4,"m":2,"q":"Define polynuclear hydrocarbons and classify them with examples.","hi":"दो या ज्यादा benzene ring; isolated (biphenyl) और fused (naphthalene)","kp":[{"t":"Hydrocarbons with two or more benzene rings","k":["two or more","multiple rings","polynuclear"]},{"t":"Isolated/non-fused: biphenyl, diphenylmethane, triphenylmethane","k":["isolated","non fused","biphenyl","diphenylmethane"]},{"t":"Fused: naphthalene, anthracene, phenanthrene","k":["fused","naphthalene","anthracene","phenanthrene"]}],"a":"Polynuclear hydrocarbons are aromatic hydrocarbons containing two or more benzene rings. (a) Isolated (non-fused) ring compounds: rings joined directly or through carbon atoms, e.g. biphenyl, diphenylmethane, triphenylmethane. (b) Fused (condensed) ring compounds: rings share two adjacent carbon atoms, e.g. naphthalene (2 rings), anthracene and phenanthrene (3 rings)."},{"id":"301S46","unit":4,"m":2,"q":"Give any four medicinally important derivatives of naphthalene with their uses.","hi":"Naproxen (NSAID), propranolol (beta blocker), naphazoline, menadione (vit K3)","kp":[{"t":"Naproxen - NSAID/analgesic","k":["naproxen","nsaid"]},{"t":"Propranolol - beta blocker","k":["propranolol","beta block"]},{"t":"Naphazoline - nasal decongestant","k":["naphazoline","decongest"]},{"t":"Menadione - vitamin K3; tolnaftate - antifungal","k":["menadione","vitamin k","tolnaftate","nafcillin"]}],"a":"1. Naproxen (6-methoxy-2-naphthylpropionic acid): NSAID, analgesic and anti-inflammatory.\n2. Propranolol (1-naphthyloxy propanolamine): non-selective beta blocker for hypertension and angina.\n3. Naphazoline: alpha agonist nasal decongestant.\n4. Menadione (2-methyl-1,4-naphthoquinone): vitamin K3, prothrombin formation.\nOthers: tolnaftate (antifungal), nafcillin (penicillin). Naphthalene itself is a moth repellent."},{"id":"301S47","unit":4,"m":2,"q":"What are the products of oxidation of naphthalene?","hi":"V2O5/air → phthalic anhydride; CrO3 → 1,4-naphthoquinone","kp":[{"t":"Air with V2O5 catalyst gives phthalic anhydride","k":["v2o5","phthalic anhydride","vanadium"]},{"t":"CrO3 in acetic acid gives 1,4-naphthoquinone","k":["naphthoquinone","cro3","chromic"]},{"t":"Alkaline KMnO4 gives phthalonic/phthalic acid","k":["kmno4","phthalic acid","phthalonic"]}],"a":"1. Catalytic air oxidation over V2O5 at high temperature breaks one ring and gives phthalic anhydride (industrial method, used for dyes, plasticisers and phenolphthalein).\n2. Chromic acid (CrO3 in glacial acetic acid) gives 1,4-naphthoquinone.\n3. Alkaline KMnO4 gives phthalonic acid, which can be converted to phthalic acid."},{"id":"301S48","unit":4,"m":2,"q":"Why are the 9 and 10 positions of anthracene most reactive?","hi":"9,10 पर reaction के बाद दो पूरे benzene ring बचते हैं","kp":[{"t":"Attack at 9,10 leaves two intact benzene rings","k":["two benzene","intact","two aromatic"]},{"t":"Least loss of resonance energy","k":["resonance energy","least loss"]},{"t":"Examples: oxidation to anthraquinone, Diels-Alder, addition of Br2","k":["anthraquinone","diels","9 10 dibromo","addition"]}],"a":"When a reagent attacks anthracene at C9 or C10, the intermediate and product retain two separate, fully aromatic benzene rings, so the loss of resonance energy is the least. Attack elsewhere leaves a naphthalene system, which is less stable than two benzene rings. Hence 9,10 positions undergo addition and substitution easily: oxidation to 9,10-anthraquinone, Diels-Alder with maleic anhydride, 9,10-addition of Br2."},{"id":"301S49","unit":4,"m":2,"q":"Write the medicinal importance of the phenanthrene nucleus.","hi":"Steroids (cyclopentanoperhydrophenanthrene) और morphine में phenanthrene nucleus","kp":[{"t":"Steroids contain cyclopentanoperhydrophenanthrene nucleus","k":["cyclopentanoperhydrophenanthrene","cyclopentano","steroid"]},{"t":"Cholesterol, sex hormones, corticosteroids, cardiac glycosides, vitamin D","k":["cholesterol","hormone","corticosteroid","cardiac glycoside","vitamin d"]},{"t":"Morphine and codeine are phenanthrene alkaloids","k":["morphine","codeine","opium"]}],"a":"Phenanthrene itself has little medicinal use, but its reduced form is present in many drugs. The steroid nucleus (cyclopentanoperhydrophenanthrene) is found in cholesterol, sex hormones (oestrogens, androgens, progesterone), corticosteroids, cardiac glycosides (digoxin), bile acids and vitamin D precursors. Morphine, codeine and related opium alkaloids contain a partially reduced phenanthrene ring system."},{"id":"301S50","unit":4,"m":2,"q":"Give the preparation and uses of diphenylmethane.","hi":"Benzene + benzyl chloride (AlCl3) → diphenylmethane; perfumery, antihistamine nucleus","kp":[{"t":"Friedel-Crafts: benzene + benzyl chloride, AlCl3","k":["benzyl chloride","alcl3","friedel"]},{"t":"Structure (C6H5)2CH2","k":["c6h5 2ch2","two phenyl"]},{"t":"Uses: perfumery; nucleus of diphenhydramine, cyclizine","k":["perfum","diphenhydramine","cyclizine","benzhydryl"]}],"a":"Diphenylmethane, (C6H5)2CH2, two phenyl rings joined by a CH2 group. Preparation: Friedel-Crafts reaction of benzene with benzyl chloride (or dichloromethane) in presence of AlCl3; or reduction of benzophenone. Uses: in perfumery (orange-like odour) and as a solvent; its diphenylmethyl (benzhydryl) group is present in antihistamines such as diphenhydramine, cyclizine and cetirizine."},{"id":"301S51","unit":4,"m":2,"q":"Give the structure and medicinal uses of anthracene derivatives.","hi":"Anthraquinone glycosides (senna) laxative; dithranol psoriasis में","kp":[{"t":"Anthraquinone glycosides (senna, cascara, aloe) as laxatives","k":["anthraquinone","senna","cascara","aloe","laxative"]},{"t":"Dithranol (anthralin) for psoriasis","k":["dithranol","anthralin","psoriasis"]},{"t":"Anthracyclines (doxorubicin) anticancer; alizarin dye","k":["doxorubicin","daunorubicin","anticancer","alizarin"]}],"a":"Anthracene is three benzene rings fused linearly, C14H10. Its oxidised derivative 9,10-anthraquinone is the nucleus of: anthraquinone glycosides of senna, cascara, aloe and rhubarb (stimulant laxatives); dithranol (anthralin, 1,8-dihydroxy-9-anthrone) used for psoriasis; anthracycline anticancer antibiotics (doxorubicin, daunorubicin) and alizarin dyes."},{"id":"301S52","unit":5,"m":5,"q":"Explain Coulson and Moffitt's modification of Baeyer's strain theory.","hi":"Cyclopropane में bent (banana) bonds, orbital angle 104°, overlap कम","kp":[{"t":"Orbitals cannot point along C-C axis in small rings","k":["internuclear axis","along the axis","line joining"]},{"t":"Bent (banana) bonds formed","k":["bent bond","banana"]},{"t":"Interorbital angle about 104 in cyclopropane","k":["104","104 degree","interorbital"]},{"t":"Less effective overlap, weaker bonds, so strain and reactivity","k":["overlap","weaker","less effective"]},{"t":"Orbitals have more p character in C-C bonds","k":["p character","more p"]}],"a":"Baeyer assumed that bond angles in cyclopropane are compressed to 60 degrees. Coulson and Moffitt (quantum mechanical approach) showed that carbon orbitals cannot be bent to 60 degrees. In cyclopropane the hybrid orbitals forming C-C bonds have more p character and the angle between them is about 104 degrees. Since this is larger than the 60 degree internuclear angle, the orbitals do not point directly at each other; they overlap at an angle outside the C-C line, forming bent or 'banana' bonds. Such overlap is less effective, so C-C bonds are weaker and the ring is strained and reactive (ring-opening reactions). Strain is thus explained as poor orbital overlap rather than actual compression to 60 degrees."},{"id":"301S53","unit":5,"m":5,"q":"Explain the action of halogens and halogen acids on cycloalkanes. How does cyclopropane differ from cyclobutane?","hi":"Cyclopropane: Br2/HBr से ring खुलता (addition); cyclobutane व बड़े ring substitution","kp":[{"t":"Cyclopropane + Br2 gives 1,3-dibromopropane (ring opening)","k":["1 3 dibromopropane","dibromopropane","ring open"]},{"t":"Cyclopropane + HBr gives 1-bromopropane (n-propyl bromide)","k":["1 bromopropane","propyl bromide","hbr"]},{"t":"Cl2 in light gives substitution (chlorocyclopropane)","k":["chlorocyclopropane","light","substitution"]},{"t":"Cyclobutane gives substitution (bromocyclobutane); no HBr reaction","k":["bromocyclobutane","chlorocyclobutane","cyclobutane"]},{"t":"Cyclopentane/cyclohexane behave like alkanes (free radical substitution)","k":["cyclopentane","cyclohexane","alkane"]}],"a":"Cyclopropane is highly strained and behaves partly like an alkene:\n- Br2 (in CCl4, dark) or Cl2 with FeCl3 opens the ring by addition: cyclopropane + Br2 -> BrCH2CH2CH2Br (1,3-dibromopropane).\n- With Cl2 in sunlight, free-radical substitution also occurs, giving chlorocyclopropane.\n- HBr (or HI) opens the ring: cyclopropane + HBr -> CH3CH2CH2Br (1-bromopropane).\nCyclobutane is less strained: with Cl2 or Br2 in light it gives substitution products (chloro- or bromocyclobutane) without ring opening, and it does not react with HBr under ordinary conditions.\nCyclopentane, cyclohexane and larger rings are strain-free in practice and undergo only free-radical substitution like open-chain alkanes."},{"id":"301S54","unit":5,"m":5,"q":"Describe chair and boat conformations of cyclohexane. Explain axial and equatorial bonds.","hi":"Chair ज्यादा stable (staggered); 6 axial + 6 equatorial H; bulky group equatorial","kp":[{"t":"Cyclohexane is puckered with normal 109.5 tetrahedral angles","k":["puckered","109","non planar"]},{"t":"Chair form most stable: all bonds staggered","k":["chair","staggered"]},{"t":"Boat form less stable: eclipsed bonds and flagpole interaction","k":["boat","eclipsed","flagpole"]},{"t":"Axial bonds parallel to ring axis (6), equatorial bonds around the ring (6)","k":["axial","equatorial"]},{"t":"Bulky substituent prefers equatorial (1,3-diaxial interaction)","k":["1 3 diaxial","diaxial","prefer equatorial"]},{"t":"Ring flipping interconverts axial and equatorial","k":["ring flip","interconver","flip"]}],"a":"Cyclohexane is not planar; it puckers so that all C-C-C angles are the normal tetrahedral 109.5 degrees, making it strain-free (as suggested by Sachse and Mohr).\nChair conformation: most stable; all C-H bonds on adjacent carbons are staggered, so no torsional strain.\nBoat conformation: less stable (by about 7 kcal/mol) because of eclipsed bonds at the sides and steric repulsion between the two 'flagpole' hydrogens. The twist-boat is intermediate.\nIn the chair, each carbon has one axial bond (parallel to the ring axis, alternately up and down; 6 in total) and one equatorial bond (pointing outward around the ring's equator; 6 in total). Ring flipping converts axial into equatorial bonds. A bulky substituent prefers the equatorial position to avoid 1,3-diaxial interactions (e.g. equatorial methylcyclohexane is more stable)."},{"id":"301S55","unit":5,"m":2,"q":"What is angle strain? Calculate the angle strain in cyclopropane according to Baeyer.","hi":"Deviation d = ½(109°28′ − ring angle); cyclopropane = 24°44′","kp":[{"t":"Deviation from tetrahedral angle 109 28' causes strain","k":["109 28","109.5","tetrahedral"]},{"t":"d = 1/2 (109 28' - ring angle)","k":["1 2","half","formula"]},{"t":"Cyclopropane: 1/2 (109 28' - 60) = 24 44'","k":["24 44","24.7","24"]}],"a":"Angle strain is the instability of a ring due to deviation of its bond angles from the tetrahedral angle (109 deg 28'). Baeyer's deviation per bond: d = 1/2 (109 deg 28' - internal angle of planar ring). For cyclopropane (60 deg): d = 1/2 (109 deg 28' - 60 deg) = 24 deg 44'. This large strain explains its high reactivity."},{"id":"301S56","unit":5,"m":2,"q":"Write any three limitations of Baeyer's strain theory.","hi":"Rings planar नहीं; cyclohexane strainless; बड़े ring भी stable","kp":[{"t":"Assumed all rings are planar; larger rings are puckered","k":["planar","puckered","non planar"]},{"t":"Predicted cyclohexane strained, but it is strain-free and most stable","k":["cyclohexane","strain free","strainless"]},{"t":"Large rings should be very unstable but are stable (heat of combustion)","k":["large ring","heat of combustion","higher ring"]}],"a":"1. Baeyer assumed all rings are planar; rings larger than cyclopentane are actually puckered.\n2. He predicted cyclohexane to be strained, but it is strain-free and the most stable ring (heat of combustion per CH2 equals open-chain alkanes).\n3. He predicted large rings to be increasingly unstable, but they are stable.\n4. It cannot explain the bent bonds of small rings."},{"id":"301S57","unit":5,"m":2,"q":"How do heats of combustion indicate the relative stability of cycloalkanes?","hi":"Per CH2 heat of combustion जितनी ज्यादा, strain उतना ज्यादा","kp":[{"t":"Higher heat of combustion per CH2 means more strain/less stability","k":["per ch2","per methylene","more strain"]},{"t":"Cyclopropane 166.6, cyclobutane 164.0 kcal/mol per CH2","k":["166.6","164"]},{"t":"Cyclohexane 157.4, same as open chain alkane (strain-free)","k":["157.4","open chain","strain free"]}],"a":"The heat released on burning, per CH2 group, measures the energy content of a ring: the more the strain, the higher the value. Per CH2 (kcal/mol): cyclopropane 166.6, cyclobutane 164.0, cyclopentane 158.7, cyclohexane 157.4 and open-chain alkane 157.4. So stability is cyclohexane > cyclopentane > cyclobutane > cyclopropane, and cyclohexane is strain-free, contrary to Baeyer's prediction."},{"id":"301S58","unit":5,"m":2,"q":"Compare the action of hydrogen (catalytic hydrogenation) on cyclopropane and cyclobutane.","hi":"Cyclopropane 80 °C पर propane, cyclobutane 200 °C पर butane","kp":[{"t":"Cyclopropane + H2/Ni at about 80 C gives propane","k":["80","propane"]},{"t":"Cyclobutane + H2/Ni needs about 200 C, gives n-butane","k":["200","butane"]},{"t":"Shows cyclopropane is more strained/reactive","k":["more strain","more reactive","less stable"]}],"a":"Both rings open on catalytic hydrogenation, but under different conditions:\ncyclopropane + H2 --Ni, about 80 C--> propane (CH3CH2CH3);\ncyclobutane + H2 --Ni, about 200 C--> n-butane.\nThe milder conditions for cyclopropane show it is more strained and more reactive than cyclobutane. Cyclopentane and cyclohexane resist ring opening under these conditions."},{"id":"301S59","unit":5,"m":2,"q":"Give two methods of preparation of cyclopropane.","hi":"Freund method: 1,3-dibromopropane + Zn; Simmons-Smith: alkene + CH2I2/Zn-Cu","kp":[{"t":"Freund's method: 1,3-dibromopropane + Zn dust (or Na)","k":["freund","1 3 dibromopropane","zinc","zn"]},{"t":"Intramolecular Wurtz reaction","k":["wurtz","intramolecular"]},{"t":"Simmons-Smith: alkene + CH2I2 + Zn(Cu) / carbene addition","k":["simmons","ch2i2","carbene","diazomethane"]}],"a":"1. Freund's method (intramolecular Wurtz reaction): 1,3-dibromopropane is heated with zinc dust (or sodium): BrCH2CH2CH2Br + Zn -> cyclopropane + ZnBr2.\n2. Simmons-Smith reaction: an alkene reacts with diiodomethane (CH2I2) and zinc-copper couple; a carbenoid adds CH2 across the double bond giving a cyclopropane (ethylene gives cyclopropane). Carbene from diazomethane and light does the same."},{"id":"301S60","unit":5,"m":2,"q":"How does the existence of cis- and trans-decalin support the Sachse-Mohr theory?","hi":"Planar ring में decalin isomer नहीं होते; दो isomer मिले → puckered strainless ring","kp":[{"t":"Planar rings would allow only one decalin","k":["planar","only one"]},{"t":"Two isomers (cis and trans) isolated","k":["cis","trans","two isomer"]},{"t":"Proves cyclohexane rings are puckered/strainless (chair)","k":["puckered","strainless","chair","non planar"]}],"a":"Mohr pointed out that if cyclohexane rings are puckered (strainless), decalin (decahydronaphthalene), with two fused six-membered rings, should exist in two stable forms: cis-decalin and trans-decalin. If the rings were planar, the trans form would be impossibly strained. Both cis and trans decalin were later isolated, confirming that six-membered rings are puckered and strain-free, as proposed by the Sachse-Mohr theory."}],"long":[{"id":"301L01","unit":1,"m":10,"q":"Why is benzene aromatic? Explain Huckel's rule with examples of aromatic, antiaromatic and non-aromatic compounds.","hi":"Benzene: planar, 6 π electrons (4n+2), resonance energy 36 kcal/mol → aromatic","kp":[{"t":"Benzene planar, sp2 carbons, cyclic conjugation","k":["planar","sp2","cyclic"]},{"t":"6 pi electrons delocalised, obeys 4n+2 (n=1)","k":["6 pi","six pi","4n+2","4n 2"]},{"t":"Resonance energy about 36 kcal/mol, extra stability","k":["36","resonance energy","stabil"]},{"t":"Substitution rather than addition","k":["substitution","not addition","electrophilic substitution"]},{"t":"Equal C-C bond lengths 1.39 A","k":["1.39","equal bond"]},{"t":"Aromatic ions: cyclopentadienyl anion, tropylium cation","k":["cyclopentadienyl","tropylium","cyclopropenyl"]},{"t":"Antiaromatic: cyclobutadiene (4n pi, planar)","k":["antiaromatic","cyclobutadiene","4n pi"]},{"t":"Non-aromatic: cyclooctatetraene (tub shaped, non-planar)","k":["cyclooctatetraene","tub","non aromatic","nonaromatic"]}],"a":{"intro":"Aromaticity is the unusual stability of certain cyclic, planar, conjugated molecules containing (4n+2) delocalised pi electrons. Benzene (C6H6) is the parent aromatic compound.","points":["Why benzene is aromatic (1) - structure: all six carbons are sp2 hybridised; the ring is a flat regular hexagon with 120 degree angles.","Why benzene is aromatic (2) - complete conjugation: each carbon has an unhybridised p orbital; the six p orbitals overlap continuously round the ring forming pi clouds above and below the plane.","Why benzene is aromatic (3) - electron count: benzene has 6 pi electrons, which fits 4n+2 with n = 1.","Aromatic characters of benzene: high resonance energy (about 36 kcal/mol from heats of hydrogenation, 49.8 observed vs 85.8 expected), equal C-C bond lengths of 1.39 A, preference for electrophilic substitution over addition, resistance to oxidation, and a ring current giving downfield NMR signals (about 7.3 ppm).","Huckel's rule: a monocyclic, planar, fully conjugated compound is aromatic if it has (4n+2) pi electrons, n = 0, 1, 2, 3 (i.e. 2, 6, 10, 14...).","Aromatic examples: benzene (6), naphthalene (10), anthracene and phenanthrene (14), cyclopropenyl cation (2), cyclopentadienyl anion (6), tropylium (cycloheptatrienyl) cation (6), and heterocycles such as pyridine, pyrrole, furan and thiophene (6).","Antiaromatic compounds: cyclic, planar, conjugated with 4n pi electrons; they are less stable than open-chain analogues. Example: cyclobutadiene (4 pi electrons), cyclopentadienyl cation (4).","Non-aromatic compounds: fail any condition, e.g. cyclooctatetraene (8 pi electrons) is tub-shaped and non-planar so it behaves like an ordinary polyene; cyclohexene and cyclopentadiene lack complete conjugation."],"diagram":"Draw benzene as a hexagon with a circle inside; beside it draw the p orbital picture (six vertical p orbitals on a flat hexagon merging into a pi cloud above and below). Also draw cyclopentadienyl anion, tropylium cation, cyclobutadiene (square) and tub-shaped cyclooctatetraene with their pi electron counts.","conclusion":"Benzene is aromatic because it is cyclic, planar, fully conjugated and has 6 pi electrons obeying Huckel's (4n+2) rule, which gives it large resonance stabilisation."}},{"id":"301L02","unit":1,"m":10,"q":"What is the effect of substituents on reactivity and orientation of electrophilic substitution in monosubstituted benzene? Explain with suitable examples.","hi":"Electron donating group: activating + o/p; electron withdrawing: deactivating + meta","kp":[{"t":"Activating groups donate electrons (+I/+R), react faster","k":["activat","electron donat","+r","+i"]},{"t":"Deactivating groups withdraw electrons (-I/-R), react slower","k":["deactivat","withdraw","-r","-i"]},{"t":"o/p directors: -OH, -NH2, -OCH3, -CH3","k":["ortho para","o p","oh","nh2","ch3"]},{"t":"Meta directors: -NO2, -CN, -COOH, -CHO, -SO3H","k":["meta","no2","cooh","so3h"]},{"t":"Halogens: deactivating but o/p directing","k":["halogen","chlorobenzene"]},{"t":"Stability of arenium ion (sigma complex) decides orientation","k":["arenium","sigma complex","intermediate"]},{"t":"Example: toluene nitration gives o- and p-nitrotoluene","k":["nitrotoluene","toluene"]},{"t":"Example: nitrobenzene gives m-dinitrobenzene","k":["dinitrobenzene","m dinitro"]}],"a":{"intro":"A group already present on the benzene ring affects (a) how fast the ring undergoes electrophilic substitution (reactivity) and (b) the position at which the new group enters (orientation). Both are explained by the electronic effects of the group on the arenium ion intermediate.","points":["Electronic effects: groups act by the inductive effect (+I or -I) and the resonance/mesomeric effect (+R or -R).","Activating groups: donate electrons and make the ring react faster than benzene. Strong: -NH2, -NHR, -NR2, -OH, -O-; moderate: -OCH3, -NHCOCH3; weak: -CH3, alkyl, -C6H5. Phenol is brominated by bromine water without catalyst to give 2,4,6-tribromophenol.","Deactivating groups: withdraw electrons and make the ring react slower. -NO2, -N+(CH3)3, -CF3, -CN, -SO3H, -CHO, -COR, -COOH, -COOR. Nitrobenzene needs fuming HNO3 and higher temperature for further nitration.","Ortho/para directors: all activating groups and halogens. Example: nitration of toluene gives a mixture of o-nitrotoluene and p-nitrotoluene; chlorination of anisole gives o- and p-chloroanisole.","Meta directors: all strongly deactivating groups except halogens. Example: nitration of nitrobenzene gives m-dinitrobenzene; nitration of benzoic acid gives m-nitrobenzoic acid.","Theory for o/p direction: in ortho and para attack, one resonance form of the arenium ion carries the + charge on the carbon bearing the substituent. An electron-donating group (lone pair of -OH/-NH2, or +I/hyperconjugation of -CH3) stabilises this form, so o/p attack is faster. Meta attack gives no such extra stabilisation.","Theory for meta direction: an electron-withdrawing group strongly destabilises the form in which + charge sits next to it (o/p attack). Meta attack avoids this, so the meta intermediate is the least destabilised.","Halogens: strong -I effect deactivates the ring, but the +R effect of their lone pairs stabilises the o/p arenium ion, so chlorobenzene reacts slower than benzene yet gives o- and p-products.","Ortho/para ratio: para is usually favoured with bulky groups because of steric hindrance at the ortho position (e.g. tert-butylbenzene gives mainly para product)."],"diagram":"Draw the three resonance structures of the arenium ion for ortho, meta and para attack on phenol (or toluene) and on nitrobenzene. Mark the structure where + charge lies on the carbon bearing the substituent: extra oxonium form for -OH (stabilised), unfavourable form next to -NO2 (destabilised).","conclusion":"Electron-donating groups are activating and ortho/para directing; electron-withdrawing groups are deactivating and meta directing; halogens are the exception (deactivating, o/p directing). This guides the planned synthesis of disubstituted benzenes."}},{"id":"301L03","unit":1,"m":10,"q":"Write the structure, preparation and uses of DDT, BHC, saccharin and chloramine.","hi":"DDT, BHC = insecticide; saccharin = sweetener; chloramine-T = antiseptic","kp":[{"t":"DDT: p,p'-dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane","k":["dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane","dichloro diphenyl","ddt"]},{"t":"DDT from chlorobenzene + chloral, conc. H2SO4","k":["chloral","ccl3cho","chlorobenzene"]},{"t":"BHC: hexachlorocyclohexane from benzene + 3Cl2 in UV light","k":["hexachlorocyclohexane","c6h6cl6","uv","sunlight"]},{"t":"Gamma isomer = gammexane/lindane (scabies, lice)","k":["gammexane","lindane","gamma","scabies"]},{"t":"Saccharin: o-sulphobenzimide from toluene","k":["sulphobenzimide","sulfobenzimide","toluene"]},{"t":"Saccharin: artificial sweetener for diabetics","k":["sweeten","diabet"]},{"t":"Chloramine-T: sodium N-chloro-p-toluenesulphonamide","k":["toluenesulphonamide","toluenesulfonamide","n chloro"]},{"t":"Chloramine: antiseptic / water disinfectant (active chlorine)","k":["antiseptic","disinfect","active chlorine"]}],"a":{"intro":"DDT, BHC, saccharin and chloramine are important benzene-derived compounds included in the syllabus for their structures and uses: two insecticides, one artificial sweetener and one antiseptic.","points":["DDT - structure: p,p'-dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane, (p-Cl-C6H4)2CH-CCl3.","DDT - preparation: 2 C6H5Cl + CCl3CHO (chloral) --conc. H2SO4--> DDT + H2O (electrophilic substitution of two chlorobenzene rings by protonated chloral).","DDT - uses and drawbacks: powerful contact and stomach insecticide used against mosquitoes (malaria), lice and agricultural pests; non-biodegradable, fat soluble, accumulates in food chains (biomagnification), so banned or restricted.","BHC - structure and preparation: benzene hexachloride is 1,2,3,4,5,6-hexachlorocyclohexane, C6H6Cl6; made by free-radical addition of 3 Cl2 to benzene in sunlight/UV light. It is a saturated compound (not aromatic).","BHC - isomers and uses: exists in several stereoisomers; the gamma isomer (gammexane, lindane) is the active insecticide; lindane lotion is used for scabies and pediculosis (lice).","Saccharin - structure and preparation: o-sulphobenzimide. Toluene + ClSO3H -> o-toluenesulphonyl chloride; + NH3 -> o-toluenesulphonamide; KMnO4 oxidises CH3 to COOH; heating removes water and cyclises to saccharin.","Saccharin - uses: non-nutritive sweetener several hundred times sweeter than sucrose, excreted unchanged; used for diabetics and obese persons; sodium saccharin (water soluble) in syrups, chewable tablets and toothpastes.","Chloramine-T - structure and preparation: CH3-C6H4-SO2-N(Cl)Na, sodium salt of N-chloro-p-toluenesulphonamide; made by treating p-toluenesulphonamide with sodium hypochlorite.","Chloramine - uses: liberates active chlorine slowly; used as antiseptic for wounds, disinfectant for water and utensils, and as an oxidising agent in analysis. Inorganic chloramine (NH2Cl) is used in water treatment."],"diagram":"Draw: DDT as CCl3-CH bonded to two para-chlorophenyl rings; BHC as a cyclohexane ring with one Cl on each carbon; saccharin as benzene fused with a five-membered ring containing C=O, NH and SO2; chloramine-T as a benzene ring with CH3 at one end and SO2N(Cl)Na at para position.","conclusion":"DDT and BHC are chlorinated insecticides (BHC's gamma isomer is used medicinally for scabies), saccharin is a calorie-free sweetener and chloramine-T is a chlorine-releasing antiseptic."}},{"id":"301L04","unit":1,"m":10,"q":"Explain electrophilic aromatic substitution. Discuss the mechanism of nitration, sulphonation and halogenation of benzene and write Friedel-Crafts alkylation and acylation reactions.","hi":"Electrophile बनना → sigma complex → H+ निकलना; NO2+, SO3, Cl+, R+, RCO+","kp":[{"t":"General: electrophile attacks pi cloud forming arenium ion, then loses H+","k":["arenium","sigma complex","wheland"]},{"t":"Rate-determining step is formation of sigma complex","k":["rate determin","slow step"]},{"t":"Nitration: HNO3 + H2SO4, electrophile NO2+","k":["nitronium","no2+"]},{"t":"Sulphonation: fuming H2SO4, electrophile SO3, reversible","k":["so3","oleum","fuming","reversib"]},{"t":"Halogenation: Cl2/FeCl3, Cl+ electrophile","k":["fecl3","cl+","lewis acid"]},{"t":"FC alkylation: R-Cl + AlCl3, carbocation R+","k":["alkylation","carbocation","r+"]},{"t":"FC acylation: RCOCl + AlCl3, acylium ion, gives ketone","k":["acylium","acetophenone","acylation"]},{"t":"Products: nitrobenzene, benzenesulphonic acid, chlorobenzene, toluene/ethylbenzene","k":["nitrobenzene","benzenesulphonic","benzenesulfonic","chlorobenzene"]}],"a":{"intro":"Electrophilic aromatic substitution (EAS) is the replacement of a hydrogen atom of the aromatic ring by an electrophile, the ring keeping its aromaticity. It is the most typical reaction of benzene because addition would destroy the stable aromatic sextet.","points":["General mechanism step 1: generation of the electrophile (E+) from the reagent, usually with an acid or Lewis acid catalyst.","Step 2 (slow, rate-determining): E+ attacks the pi electron cloud, forming a resonance-stabilised carbocation called the arenium ion or sigma complex; the attacked carbon becomes sp3.","Step 3 (fast): a base removes H+ from the sp3 carbon, restoring the aromatic ring and giving C6H5-E.","Nitration: benzene + conc. HNO3 + conc. H2SO4 at about 50-60 C. HNO3 + 2H2SO4 -> NO2+ + H3O+ + 2HSO4-. NO2+ (nitronium ion) attacks the ring; HSO4- removes H+, giving nitrobenzene.","Sulphonation: benzene + fuming H2SO4 (or hot conc. H2SO4). 2H2SO4 <-> SO3 + H3O+ + HSO4-. SO3 is the electrophile; product benzenesulphonic acid. Reaction is reversible: heating with dilute acid/steam regenerates benzene.","Halogenation: benzene + Cl2 (or Br2) with FeCl3/FeBr3/AlCl3 in the dark. Cl2 + FeCl3 -> Cl+ + FeCl4-. Cl+ forms the sigma complex; FeCl4- takes H+ to give chlorobenzene + HCl, regenerating FeCl3. Reactivity F2 > Cl2 > Br2 > I2; iodination needs HNO3.","Friedel-Crafts alkylation: C6H6 + CH3Cl --anhyd. AlCl3--> C6H5CH3 + HCl. AlCl3 generates carbocation R+ (CH3-Cl->AlCl3 complex). Limitations: polyalkylation, carbocation rearrangement, fails with deactivated rings and aryl halides.","Friedel-Crafts acylation: C6H6 + CH3COCl --anhyd. AlCl3--> C6H5COCH3 (acetophenone) + HCl. Electrophile acylium ion CH3-C≡O+; no rearrangement and no polyacylation.","Energy profile: EAS shows two transition states with the arenium ion as intermediate; the first (forming the sigma complex) has the higher energy."],"diagram":"Draw the general mechanism: benzene + E+ -> three resonance forms of the arenium ion (positive charge at ortho and para positions to the sp3 carbon) -> loss of H+ -> substituted benzene. Also draw the energy profile with two humps and the arenium ion in the valley.","conclusion":"All these reactions follow the same addition-elimination path through the arenium ion; they differ only in how the electrophile (NO2+, SO3, Cl+, R+, RCO+) is generated."}},{"id":"301L05","unit":1,"m":10,"q":"Write the structure, uses and preparation of chlorobenzene, phenol, benzoic acid and benzaldehyde.","hi":"चारों benzene derivatives: preparation reactions + uses याद करो","kp":[{"t":"Chlorobenzene: benzene + Cl2/FeCl3 or Sandmeyer","k":["chlorobenzene","sandmeyer","fecl3"]},{"t":"Chlorobenzene uses: solvent, DDT, phenol manufacture","k":["solvent","ddt"]},{"t":"Phenol: cumene process / NaOH fusion of benzenesulphonic acid","k":["cumene","fusion","benzenesulphonic","benzenesulfonic"]},{"t":"Phenol uses: antiseptic (carbolic acid), bakelite, aspirin","k":["carbolic","bakelite","antiseptic","aspirin"]},{"t":"Benzoic acid: oxidation of toluene with KMnO4","k":["kmno4","oxidation of toluene","toluene"]},{"t":"Benzoic acid uses: preservative (sodium benzoate), Whitfield ointment","k":["preservativ","sodium benzoate","whitfield","antifungal"]},{"t":"Benzaldehyde: Etard reaction / Gattermann-Koch / Rosenmund","k":["etard","gattermann","rosenmund","chromyl"]},{"t":"Benzaldehyde uses: flavour (bitter almond), dyes, perfumery","k":["almond","flavour","flavor","perfum"]}],"a":{"intro":"Chlorobenzene (C6H5Cl), phenol (C6H5OH), benzoic acid (C6H5COOH) and benzaldehyde (C6H5CHO) are monosubstituted benzene derivatives widely used as intermediates in drug and dye synthesis.","points":["Chlorobenzene - preparation: (a) benzene + Cl2 with FeCl3 (electrophilic substitution); (b) Sandmeyer reaction: benzenediazonium chloride + CuCl/HCl -> chlorobenzene + N2.","Chlorobenzene - uses: solvent; manufacture of DDT, phenol (Dow process: NaOH at high temperature and pressure), aniline and nitrochlorobenzenes for drugs and dyes.","Phenol - preparation: (a) cumene process: cumene + O2 -> cumene hydroperoxide; with dilute acid -> phenol + acetone; (b) fusion of sodium benzenesulphonate with NaOH then acidification; (c) warming benzenediazonium chloride with water; (d) chlorobenzene + NaOH at high temperature and pressure.","Phenol - uses: carbolic acid as antiseptic and disinfectant (standard in phenol coefficient test); manufacture of bakelite (phenol-formaldehyde resin), salicylic acid and aspirin, picric acid, dyes and nylon intermediates.","Benzoic acid - preparation: (a) oxidation of toluene with alkaline KMnO4 (or chromic acid); (b) hydrolysis of benzonitrile with acid/alkali; (c) phenylmagnesium bromide + CO2 then H3O+; (d) hydrolysis of benzotrichloride.","Benzoic acid - uses: preservative (sodium benzoate in foods and syrups), antifungal in Whitfield's ointment (with salicylic acid), manufacture of benzoyl chloride, benzocaine-type esters and dyes.","Benzaldehyde - preparation: (a) Etard reaction: toluene + chromyl chloride (CrO2Cl2) then hydrolysis; (b) hydrolysis of benzal chloride (C6H5CHCl2); (c) Gattermann-Koch: benzene + CO + HCl with AlCl3/CuCl; (d) Rosenmund reduction of benzoyl chloride (H2, Pd/BaSO4).","Benzaldehyde - uses: flavouring agent (oil of bitter almond), perfumery, manufacture of malachite green dye, cinnamic acid and mandelic acid.","Structures: each is a benzene ring carrying one group: -Cl, -OH, -COOH or -CHO."],"diagram":"Draw four benzene rings bearing -Cl, -OH, -COOH and -CHO, with an arrow scheme for one key preparation of each (benzene -> chlorobenzene with Cl2/FeCl3; cumene -> phenol + acetone; toluene -> benzoic acid with KMnO4; toluene -> benzaldehyde with CrO2Cl2).","conclusion":"These four compounds are prepared mainly from benzene or toluene and serve as starting materials for many drugs, preservatives, antiseptics and dyes."}},{"id":"301L06","unit":2,"m":10,"q":"Discuss the acidity of phenols. Compare it with alcohols and carboxylic acids, explain the effect of substituents on acidity, and give the qualitative tests for phenols.","hi":"Phenol acidity: phenoxide resonance; EWG ↑, EDG ↓; tests: FeCl3, Br2 water, Liebermann","kp":[{"t":"Phenol ionises to phenoxide, stabilised by resonance","k":["phenoxide","resonance"]},{"t":"Order: carboxylic acid > phenol > water > alcohol","k":["carboxylic acid","alcohol","order"]},{"t":"pKa values: phenol ~10, ethanol ~16, acetic/benzoic acid ~4-5","k":["pka","10","16"]},{"t":"EWG (-NO2) increase acidity; picric acid very strong","k":["no2","nitrophenol","picric"]},{"t":"EDG (-CH3, -OCH3) decrease acidity (cresols)","k":["cresol","ch3","electron donat"]},{"t":"Ferric chloride test: violet colour","k":["violet","fecl3","ferric chloride"]},{"t":"Bromine water: white ppt of 2,4,6-tribromophenol","k":["tribromophenol","white precipitate","bromine water"]},{"t":"Liebermann nitroso test / phthalein test","k":["liebermann","phthalein","nitroso"]}],"a":{"intro":"Phenols are compounds with an -OH group directly attached to an aromatic ring. They are weak acids that ionise in water to give phenoxide ion and H+: C6H5OH <-> C6H5O- + H+. Phenol is more acidic than alcohols but less acidic than carboxylic acids.","points":["Reason for acidity: the phenoxide ion is stabilised by resonance; its negative charge is delocalised to the ortho and para carbons of the ring. Phenol itself also shows resonance, but with charge separation, so the anion gains more stability.","Comparison with alcohols: alkoxide ions (e.g. ethoxide) have no resonance and the +I effect of the alkyl group destabilises them. Phenol pKa about 10, ethanol about 16; phenol dissolves in NaOH, ethanol does not.","Comparison with carboxylic acids: in the carboxylate ion the negative charge is shared equally by two electronegative oxygen atoms (two equivalent resonance forms), whereas in phenoxide it is mainly on less electronegative carbons. Benzoic acid pKa 4.2. Phenol does not liberate CO2 from NaHCO3.","Electron-withdrawing substituents increase acidity: -NO2, -CN, -CHO, halogens stabilise the phenoxide ion. Nitrophenols: p-nitrophenol (pKa about 7.1) and o-nitrophenol are more acidic than m-nitrophenol because only at o/p can the charge delocalise onto NO2. 2,4,6-Trinitrophenol (picric acid) is almost as strong as a mineral acid.","Electron-donating substituents decrease acidity: -CH3, -OCH3, -NH2 intensify the negative charge. Cresols are less acidic than phenol.","Ferric chloride test: neutral FeCl3 gives violet (purple) colour with phenol (green with catechol, blue-violet with resorcinol type compounds varies).","Bromine water test: white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenol.","Liebermann's nitroso test: phenol + NaNO2 + conc. H2SO4 gives a deep green or blue colour, which turns red on dilution with water and blue again with NaOH.","Phthalein test: phenol heated with phthalic anhydride and conc. H2SO4, then made alkaline with NaOH, gives pink-red colour (phenolphthalein).","Solubility test: phenol dissolves in NaOH (forms sodium phenoxide) but not in NaHCO3, distinguishing it from carboxylic acids."],"diagram":"Draw the resonance structures of phenoxide ion showing negative charge on O and at ortho and para carbons; also draw p-nitrophenoxide with the charge delocalised onto the NO2 oxygen.","conclusion":"Phenols are weak acids because of resonance stabilisation of the phenoxide ion; electron-withdrawing groups raise and electron-donating groups lower their acidity, and FeCl3, bromine water and Liebermann tests identify them."}},{"id":"301L07","unit":2,"m":10,"q":"Classify phenols. Give the general methods of preparation and chemical reactions of phenol.","hi":"Mono, di, trihydric phenols; cumene process; Kolbe, Reimer-Tiemann, bromination","kp":[{"t":"Monohydric: phenol, cresols, naphthols","k":["monohydric","one oh","mono hydric"]},{"t":"Dihydric: catechol, resorcinol, hydroquinone","k":["dihydric","catechol","resorcinol","hydroquinone","quinol"]},{"t":"Trihydric: pyrogallol, phloroglucinol","k":["trihydric","pyrogallol","phloroglucinol"]},{"t":"Preparation: cumene process (phenol + acetone)","k":["cumene","cumene hydroperoxide","isopropylbenzene"]},{"t":"Preparation: from benzenesulphonic acid / diazonium salt / chlorobenzene","k":["benzenesulphonic","benzenesulfonic","diazonium","dow"]},{"t":"Reaction: Kolbe-Schmitt gives salicylic acid","k":["kolbe","salicylic acid"]},{"t":"Reaction: Reimer-Tiemann gives salicylaldehyde","k":["reimer","salicylaldehyde"]},{"t":"Ring substitution: bromination, nitration (picric acid)","k":["tribromophenol","picric","nitrophenol"]},{"t":"OH reactions: esterification, Williamson ether, Schotten-Baumann","k":["ester","ether","schotten","anisole"]}],"a":{"intro":"Phenols are hydroxy derivatives of aromatic hydrocarbons in which the -OH group is directly attached to the ring carbon. Phenol, C6H5OH (carbolic acid), is the simplest member.","points":["Classification by number of OH groups: (a) monohydric - phenol, o-/m-/p-cresol, alpha- and beta-naphthol; (b) dihydric - catechol (1,2-), resorcinol (1,3-), hydroquinone/quinol (1,4-dihydroxybenzene); (c) trihydric - pyrogallol (1,2,3-), hydroxyquinol (1,2,4-), phloroglucinol (1,3,5-trihydroxybenzene).","Preparation 1 - cumene process (industrial): benzene + propene -> cumene; air oxidation gives cumene hydroperoxide; dilute acid splits it into phenol and acetone.","Preparation 2 - from benzenesulphonic acid: sodium benzenesulphonate fused with NaOH at about 300 C gives sodium phenoxide; acidification gives phenol.","Preparation 3 - from diazonium salts: benzenediazonium chloride warmed with water gives phenol + N2 + HCl.","Preparation 4 - from chlorobenzene (Dow process): heating with aqueous NaOH at high temperature and pressure, then acidification.","Reactions of OH group: acidic nature (forms sodium phenoxide with NaOH); esterification/acylation with acetyl or benzoyl chloride (Schotten-Baumann gives phenyl benzoate); Williamson synthesis gives anisole; zinc dust distillation reduces phenol to benzene.","Ring substitution (OH strongly activating, o/p): bromine water gives 2,4,6-tribromophenol; Br2 in CS2 at low temperature gives p-bromophenol; dilute HNO3 gives o- and p-nitrophenol; conc. HNO3 gives picric acid; sulphonation gives o- (low temp) or p-phenolsulphonic acid (high temp).","Kolbe-Schmitt reaction: sodium phenoxide + CO2 (about 125 C, pressure) -> salicylic acid.","Reimer-Tiemann reaction: phenol + CHCl3 + NaOH -> salicylaldehyde (via dichlorocarbene).","Other reactions: coupling with diazonium salts gives azo dyes; condensation with formaldehyde gives bakelite; oxidation with chromic acid gives p-benzoquinone; catalytic hydrogenation gives cyclohexanol; with phthalic anhydride gives phenolphthalein."],"diagram":"Draw a flow chart with phenol in the centre and arrows to: sodium phenoxide (NaOH), anisole (CH3I), phenyl benzoate (C6H5COCl/NaOH), 2,4,6-tribromophenol (Br2 water), picric acid (conc. HNO3), salicylic acid (CO2, Kolbe), salicylaldehyde (CHCl3/NaOH), p-hydroxyazobenzene (diazonium salt), benzene (Zn dust).","conclusion":"Phenol is prepared industrially by the cumene process; its reactions involve both the acidic OH group and the highly activated ring, giving important products such as salicylic acid, picric acid and bakelite."}},{"id":"301L08","unit":2,"m":10,"q":"Discuss the basicity of aromatic amines and the effect of substituents on it. Give the methods of preparation and reactions of aniline.","hi":"Aniline lone pair resonance से कम basic; Sn/HCl reduction; acetylation, diazotisation","kp":[{"t":"Basicity: lone pair delocalised into ring, aniline weaker base than NH3","k":["lone pair","delocali","weaker base"]},{"t":"pKb values: aniline 9.4, NH3 4.75, methylamine 3.4","k":["9.4","4.75","pkb"]},{"t":"EDG increase basicity (p-toluidine, p-anisidine)","k":["toluidine","anisidine","electron donat"]},{"t":"EWG decrease basicity (p-nitroaniline); ortho effect","k":["nitroaniline","withdraw","ortho effect"]},{"t":"Preparation: reduction of nitrobenzene with Sn/HCl or Fe/HCl","k":["sn hcl","fe hcl","reduction of nitrobenzene","nitrobenzene"]},{"t":"Preparation: Hofmann bromamide from benzamide","k":["hofmann","bromamide","benzamide"]},{"t":"Reactions: acetylation (acetanilide), bromination (tribromoaniline)","k":["acetanilide","tribromoaniline"]},{"t":"Diazotisation, carbylamine test, sulphonation (sulphanilic acid)","k":["diazoti","carbylamine","isocyanide","sulphanilic","sulfanilic"]}],"a":{"intro":"Aromatic amines have an amino group attached directly to the aromatic ring; aniline (C6H5NH2) is the simplest. Like all amines they are bases because of the lone pair on nitrogen, but they are much weaker bases than aliphatic amines.","points":["Basicity of aniline: the nitrogen lone pair is delocalised into the ring (resonance), so it is less available for protonation. Aniline is resonance stabilised but the anilinium ion is not; the sp2 ring carbon is also electron-withdrawing.","Comparison: CH3NH2 (pKb 3.4) > NH3 (4.75) > C6H5NH2 (9.4) > diphenylamine (very weak) > triphenylamine (practically neutral).","Electron-donating substituents (-CH3, -OCH3, -NH2) at para position raise electron density on N and increase basicity: p-anisidine > p-toluidine > aniline.","Electron-withdrawing substituents (-NO2, -CN, -Cl, -COOH) reduce basicity; the effect is greatest at ortho/para because of resonance, so p-nitroaniline is about ten thousand times weaker than aniline.","Ortho effect: nearly all ortho-substituted anilines (even o-toluidine) are weaker bases than aniline because of steric hindrance to solvation of the anilinium ion.","Preparation: (a) reduction of nitrobenzene with Sn/HCl or Fe/HCl (industrial), or H2 with Ni/Pd; (b) Hofmann bromamide reaction: benzamide + Br2 + NaOH -> aniline; (c) ammonolysis of chlorobenzene with NH3 and Cu2O at high temperature and pressure.","Reactions as a base: forms salts (anilinium chloride) with acids; acetylation with acetic anhydride gives acetanilide; benzoylation (Schotten-Baumann) gives benzanilide.","Ring substitution: bromine water gives white 2,4,6-tribromoaniline; nitration is done after acetylation to obtain p-nitroaniline; sulphonation (heating aniline hydrogen sulphate) gives sulphanilic acid.","Other reactions: diazotisation (NaNO2/HCl, 0-5 C) gives benzenediazonium chloride; carbylamine test - CHCl3 + alcoholic KOH gives foul-smelling phenyl isocyanide (test for primary amines); oxidation gives coloured products."],"diagram":"Draw the resonance structures of aniline showing the lone pair moving into the ring (negative charge at ortho and para carbons, positive N), and contrast with the anilinium ion where no lone pair is left.","conclusion":"Aniline is a weak base because its lone pair is delocalised into the ring; donating groups strengthen and withdrawing groups weaken it. It is prepared by reducing nitrobenzene and is the key starting material for sulpha drugs, dyes and diazonium chemistry."}},{"id":"301L09","unit":2,"m":10,"q":"Describe the preparation of aryl diazonium salts and discuss their synthetic applications.","hi":"Diazonium salt: N2 replacement (Cl, Br, CN, F, I, OH, H) + coupling (azo dyes)","kp":[{"t":"Diazotisation: ArNH2 + NaNO2 + HCl at 0-5 C","k":["diazoti","nano2","0 5"]},{"t":"Stability of aryl diazonium due to resonance with ring","k":["resonance","stabil"]},{"t":"Sandmeyer: CuCl, CuBr, CuCN gives ArCl, ArBr, ArCN","k":["sandmeyer","cucl","cucn","benzonitrile"]},{"t":"Gattermann: Cu powder + HX","k":["gattermann","copper powder"]},{"t":"KI gives iodobenzene","k":["iodobenzene","ki"]},{"t":"Balz-Schiemann: HBF4 gives fluorobenzene","k":["schiemann","hbf4","fluorobenzene","fluoroborate"]},{"t":"Warm water gives phenol; H3PO2 gives benzene","k":["phenol","h3po2","hypophosphorous","deamination"]},{"t":"Coupling with phenols/amines gives azo dyes","k":["coupling","azo","dye"]}],"a":{"intro":"Aryl diazonium salts (Ar-N2+ X-) are prepared from primary aromatic amines by diazotisation. Because N2 is an excellent leaving group, they are versatile intermediates for introducing many groups into the benzene ring that cannot be introduced directly.","points":["Preparation: aniline is dissolved in excess HCl, cooled to 0-5 C and treated with aqueous NaNO2: C6H5NH2 + NaNO2 + 2HCl -> C6H5N2+Cl- + NaCl + 2H2O. The electrophile is the nitrosonium ion (NO+). The solution is used immediately.","Stability: aryl diazonium ions are stable at low temperature because the positive charge is delocalised into the ring; aliphatic diazonium ions decompose at once.","Replacement by halogen and cyano (Sandmeyer): with CuCl/HCl -> chlorobenzene; CuBr/HBr -> bromobenzene; CuCN/KCN -> benzonitrile (hydrolysis gives benzoic acid).","Gattermann reaction: Cu powder with HCl or HBr gives aryl chloride or bromide.","Replacement by iodine: warming with KI gives iodobenzene (no catalyst needed).","Replacement by fluorine (Balz-Schiemann): HBF4 gives the diazonium fluoroborate, which on heating gives fluorobenzene + N2 + BF3.","Replacement by OH: warming with water (dilute H2SO4) gives phenol.","Replacement by H (deamination): hypophosphorous acid (H3PO2) or ethanol reduces it to benzene; used to remove an NH2 group after it has served as a directing group (e.g. synthesis of 1,3,5-tribromobenzene from aniline).","Reactions retaining nitrogen: reduction with SnCl2/HCl or Na2SO3 gives phenylhydrazine; coupling with phenol in alkaline medium gives p-hydroxyazobenzene (orange) and with aniline in weakly acidic medium gives p-aminoazobenzene (yellow); with beta-naphthol gives an orange-red dye.","Pharmaceutical relevance: azo dyes, the first sulphonamide prodrug prontosil (an azo compound), and the diazo test (Bratton-Marshall) for sulphonamide estimation rely on diazonium chemistry."],"diagram":"Draw a wheel diagram: benzenediazonium chloride in the centre with arrows to C6H5Cl (CuCl), C6H5Br (CuBr), C6H5CN (CuCN), C6H5I (KI), C6H5F (HBF4, heat), C6H5OH (H2O, warm), C6H6 (H3PO2), C6H5NHNH2 (SnCl2/HCl) and p-hydroxyazobenzene (phenol, OH-).","conclusion":"Diazonium salts are among the most useful synthetic intermediates: they let the chemist replace an NH2 group by halogens, CN, OH or H, and they give azo dyes by coupling."}},{"id":"301L10","unit":2,"m":10,"q":"Discuss the acidity of aromatic carboxylic acids and the effect of substituents on it. Give the methods of preparation and important reactions of benzoic acid.","hi":"Benzoic acid pKa 4.2; EWG ↑ acidity, EDG ↓; toluene oxidation; ester, acid chloride","kp":[{"t":"Carboxylate ion stabilised by two equivalent resonance forms","k":["carboxylate","equivalent","resonance"]},{"t":"Benzoic acid pKa 4.2, stronger than acetic acid (4.76)","k":["4.2","4.76","acetic acid"]},{"t":"EWG (-NO2, -Cl) increase acidity (p-nitrobenzoic acid)","k":["nitrobenzoic","withdraw","no2"]},{"t":"EDG (-CH3, -OCH3, -OH at para) decrease acidity","k":["toluic","methoxybenzoic","electron donat"]},{"t":"Ortho effect: o-substituted acids stronger; salicylic acid H-bond","k":["ortho effect","salicylic"]},{"t":"Preparation: oxidation of toluene by KMnO4","k":["kmno4","toluene"]},{"t":"Preparation: hydrolysis of benzonitrile / Grignard + CO2","k":["benzonitrile","grignard","co2"]},{"t":"Reactions: esterification, benzoyl chloride (SOCl2/PCl5), benzamide","k":["ester","ethyl benzoate","benzoyl chloride","socl2","benzamide"]},{"t":"Decarboxylation with soda lime; reduction LiAlH4; nitration gives m-nitrobenzoic acid","k":["soda lime","lialh4","benzyl alcohol","m nitrobenzoic"]}],"a":{"intro":"Aromatic acids contain a -COOH group attached directly to the aromatic ring. Benzoic acid (C6H5COOH) is the simplest; it is a weak acid that ionises to the benzoate ion and H+.","points":["Reason for acidity: the benzoate ion is stabilised by resonance between two equivalent structures that place the negative charge on two oxygen atoms equally.","Strength: benzoic acid (pKa 4.20) is stronger than acetic acid (4.76) because the sp2 phenyl group is electron-withdrawing compared to methyl, but weaker than formic acid (3.75).","Electron-withdrawing substituents stabilise the benzoate ion and increase acidity: p-nitrobenzoic acid (pKa about 3.4) and p-chlorobenzoic acid are stronger than benzoic acid.","Electron-donating substituents destabilise the anion and decrease acidity: p-toluic acid, p-methoxybenzoic acid (anisic acid) and p-hydroxybenzoic acid are weaker than benzoic acid.","Ortho effect: almost all ortho-substituted benzoic acids are stronger than benzoic acid (steric twisting of COOH out of the ring plane). Salicylic acid (pKa about 3.0) is especially strong because intramolecular hydrogen bonding stabilises its anion.","Preparation: (a) oxidation of toluene (or any alkylbenzene) with alkaline KMnO4 or chromic acid; (b) hydrolysis of benzonitrile; (c) phenylmagnesium bromide + CO2 (dry ice), then acid; (d) hydrolysis of benzotrichloride; (e) oxidation of benzaldehyde.","Reactions of COOH group: forms salts with NaOH and NaHCO3 (effervescence); esterification with ethanol/H2SO4 gives ethyl benzoate; SOCl2 or PCl5 gives benzoyl chloride; benzoyl chloride + NH3 gives benzamide; LiAlH4 reduces it to benzyl alcohol.","Decarboxylation: heating sodium benzoate with soda lime (NaOH + CaO) gives benzene.","Ring substitution: -COOH is deactivating and meta directing; nitration gives m-nitrobenzoic acid, bromination (FeBr3) gives m-bromobenzoic acid; it does not undergo Friedel-Crafts reactions.","Uses: sodium benzoate as food/syrup preservative; antifungal in Whitfield's ointment; benzoyl peroxide and benzocaine-type esters are derivatives."],"diagram":"Draw the two equivalent resonance structures of benzoate ion; then a reaction chart from benzoic acid to sodium benzoate, ethyl benzoate, benzoyl chloride, benzamide, benzyl alcohol, benzene (soda lime) and m-nitrobenzoic acid.","conclusion":"Benzoic acid is a moderately strong organic acid whose strength is raised by electron-withdrawing and ortho substituents and lowered by donating groups; it is made by oxidising toluene and is the parent of many esters, amides and preservatives."}},{"id":"301L11","unit":3,"m":10,"q":"Write a note on rancidity and hydrogenation of oils. Explain the principle and significance of acid value, saponification value and iodine value.","hi":"Rancidity + hardening; AV, SV, IV की definition, titration, significance","kp":[{"t":"Rancidity types: hydrolytic, oxidative, ketonic","k":["hydrolytic","oxidative","ketonic"]},{"t":"Prevention by antioxidants (BHA, BHT, tocopherol)","k":["antioxidant","bha","bht","tocopherol"]},{"t":"Hydrogenation: H2 + Ni catalyst converts oil to solid fat (vanaspati)","k":["nickel","vanaspati","hydrogenat"]},{"t":"Acid value: mg KOH for free fatty acids in 1 g, titrate with KOH/phenolphthalein","k":["acid value","free fatty acid","phenolphthalein"]},{"t":"Saponification value: reflux with 0.5 N alcoholic KOH, back titrate with HCl","k":["0.5 n","alcoholic koh","back titrat","reflux"]},{"t":"SV inversely related to molecular weight","k":["molecular weight","inversely"]},{"t":"Iodine value: g iodine per 100 g; Wijs method (ICl)","k":["wijs","icl","iodine monochloride","100 g"]},{"t":"Iodine liberated by KI titrated with sodium thiosulphate, starch indicator","k":["thiosulphate","thiosulfate","starch"]}],"a":{"intro":"Fats and oils deteriorate on storage (rancidity) and are modified industrially (hydrogenation). Their purity, identity and quality are checked by analytical constants such as acid value, saponification value and iodine value.","points":["Rancidity - definition and types: unpleasant odour and taste on storage. Hydrolytic (lipase/moisture release free fatty acids), oxidative (O2 attacks double bonds via free radicals giving hydroperoxides, aldehydes and ketones; catalysed by light, heat, Cu/Fe), ketonic (moulds produce methyl ketones).","Rancidity - prevention: cool, dark, dry storage in full airtight containers; antioxidants (BHA, BHT, propyl gallate, tocopherol); metal chelators (citric acid, EDTA).","Hydrogenation: addition of H2 to the double bonds in presence of finely divided Ni with heat and pressure; liquid oils become semi-solid fats (vanaspati, margarine); melting point rises, iodine value falls, resistance to rancidity increases; partial hydrogenation forms trans fats.","Acid value - definition: mg of KOH needed to neutralise the free fatty acids in 1 g of fat.","Acid value - principle: a weighed sample is dissolved in neutralised ethanol-ether mixture and titrated with 0.1 N KOH (or NaOH) using phenolphthalein. AV = 56.1 x N x V / W. Significance: measures free acid; high AV means hydrolytic rancidity or poor refining.","Saponification value - definition: mg of KOH needed to saponify 1 g of fat.","Saponification value - principle: the sample is refluxed with a known excess of 0.5 N alcoholic KOH until fully saponified; the unused KOH is back-titrated with 0.5 N HCl (phenolphthalein); a blank is run. SV = (blank - sample titre) x 28.05 / W. Significance: inversely proportional to mean molecular weight; high SV (coconut oil, butter) indicates short-chain acids; also detects unsaponifiable adulterants (mineral oil lowers SV).","Iodine value - definition: g of iodine absorbed by 100 g of fat.","Iodine value - principle (Wijs method): the sample in chloroform/CCl4 is treated with excess Wijs solution (iodine monochloride in glacial acetic acid) and kept in the dark; ICl adds to the double bonds. Excess ICl is treated with KI to liberate I2, which is titrated with 0.1 N sodium thiosulphate using starch; a blank is run. IV = (blank - sample) x 1.269 / W. (Hanus method uses IBr.)","Iodine value - significance: measures unsaturation; classifies oils (drying above 130, semi-drying 100-130, non-drying below 100); controls hydrogenation; detects adulteration."],"diagram":"Draw the reflux set-up for saponification value: round-bottom flask with sample and alcoholic KOH on a water bath, fitted with a vertical (reflux) condenser; and the iodine flask (stoppered conical flask) used for the iodine value.","conclusion":"Rancidity spoils oils and is prevented by antioxidants, while hydrogenation hardens them; acid, saponification and iodine values tell us their freshness, chain length and unsaturation and are used to identify oils and detect adulteration."}},{"id":"301L12","unit":3,"m":10,"q":"Differentiate between fats and oils. Describe the methods of analysis (analytical constants) of fats and oils and give their industrial and pharmaceutical importance.","hi":"Fat vs oil; AV, SV, EV, IV, acetyl, RM value; soap, vanaspati, paints, pharmacy","kp":[{"t":"Fats solid, saturated, animal; oils liquid, unsaturated, plant","k":["solid","liquid","saturated"]},{"t":"Both are triglycerides (glycerol esters of fatty acids)","k":["triglyceride","glycerol ester","glyceride"]},{"t":"Acid value and saponification value","k":["acid value","saponification value"]},{"t":"Ester value = SV - AV","k":["ester value","sv av"]},{"t":"Iodine value - unsaturation","k":["iodine value","unsaturat"]},{"t":"Acetyl value - free OH; RM value - volatile soluble acids","k":["acetyl value","reichert","rm value"]},{"t":"Industrial: soaps, glycerol, vanaspati, paints/varnishes","k":["soap","vanaspati","paint","varnish"]},{"t":"Pharmaceutical: castor oil purgative, cod liver oil vitamins, theobroma oil suppository base","k":["castor","cod liver","theobroma","suppositor","vehicle"]}],"a":{"intro":"Fats and oils are lipids that are triglycerides, i.e. esters of glycerol with long-chain fatty acids. They are distinguished by physical state and composition and are evaluated by chemical constants.","points":["Fats vs oils: fats are solid/semi-solid at room temperature, oils are liquid; fats contain mainly saturated acids (palmitic, stearic), oils mainly unsaturated acids (oleic, linoleic); oils have higher iodine values; fats are mostly animal, oils mostly vegetable (exceptions: fish oils are liquid, coconut and palm kernel are solid plant fats); oils are more prone to oxidative rancidity.","Acid value: mg KOH to neutralise free fatty acids in 1 g; titration with KOH using phenolphthalein; shows freshness/rancidity.","Saponification value: mg KOH to saponify 1 g; reflux with excess alcoholic KOH and back titrate with HCl; inversely related to mean molecular weight.","Ester value: SV - AV; amount of esterified fatty acids.","Iodine value: g iodine absorbed by 100 g; Wijs (ICl) or Hanus (IBr) method with thiosulphate back titration; measures unsaturation.","Acetyl value: mg KOH to neutralise acetic acid from saponification of 1 g acetylated fat (acetylation with acetic anhydride); measures free hydroxyl groups (high in castor oil).","Reichert-Meissl value: ml of 0.1 N alkali to neutralise steam-volatile water-soluble acids from 5 g fat; high in butter/ghee; detects adulteration. Polenske value: volatile water-insoluble acids.","Other tests: unsaponifiable matter, peroxide value (oxidative rancidity), refractive index, specific gravity, melting point.","Industrial importance: soaps and detergents (with glycerol as by-product); vanaspati and margarine by hydrogenation; drying oils for paints, varnishes, inks; lubricants, cosmetics and candles.","Pharmaceutical importance: castor oil as purgative; cod liver and shark liver oils as sources of vitamins A and D; arachis/sesame oil as vehicles for oily injections; theobroma oil as suppository base; wool fat and hydrogenated oils as ointment bases; glycerol as solvent and humectant."],"diagram":"","conclusion":"Fats and oils differ mainly in fatty acid saturation and physical state; their analytical constants identify them, check purity and detect adulteration, and they are important raw materials in industry and pharmacy."}},{"id":"301L13","unit":3,"m":10,"q":"Explain hydrolysis and saponification of fats and oils. Discuss the reactions of fatty acids and write a note on drying oils.","hi":"Hydrolysis (acid/enzyme/alkali), soap बनाना; fatty acid reactions; linseed oil drying","kp":[{"t":"Hydrolysis by acid, steam or lipase gives glycerol + fatty acids","k":["lipase","glycerol","fatty acid"]},{"t":"Saponification: alkaline hydrolysis with NaOH/KOH gives soap","k":["saponif","soap","naoh"]},{"t":"Fatty acid reactions as acids: salts, esters, acid chlorides","k":["salt","ester","acid chloride"]},{"t":"Double bond reactions: hydrogenation, halogenation","k":["hydrogenat","halogen","iodine"]},{"t":"Oxidation (KMnO4, ozonolysis) and elaidinisation","k":["kmno4","ozonolysis","elaidic","oxidat"]},{"t":"Drying oils rich in linolenic acid, e.g. linseed oil","k":["linseed","linolenic","tung"]},{"t":"Drying by autoxidation and polymerisation into a film","k":["autoxid","polymeri","film"]},{"t":"Iodine value classification: drying >130","k":["130","semi drying","non drying"]}],"a":{"intro":"Fats and oils are triglycerides; their ester linkages undergo hydrolysis, while the fatty acid chains undergo reactions of the carboxyl group and of the carbon-carbon double bonds. Highly unsaturated oils harden in air and are called drying oils.","points":["Hydrolysis: triglyceride + 3H2O -> glycerol + 3 fatty acids. It can be done by heating with water/steam under pressure, by dilute acids, or by the enzyme lipase (also causes hydrolytic rancidity).","Saponification: hydrolysis with NaOH or KOH gives glycerol and soap (sodium or potassium salts of fatty acids). Na soaps are hard, K soaps soft. Salt is added to separate soap (salting out); glycerol is recovered from the lye.","Reactions of the carboxyl group: salt formation with alkalis (soaps) and metal ions (metallic soaps such as zinc stearate); esterification with alcohols; conversion to acid chlorides with SOCl2/PCl5; reduction to fatty alcohols (used in detergents).","Hydrogenation: unsaturated acids add H2 (Ni) to give saturated acids, e.g. oleic -> stearic acid.","Halogenation: addition of I2, Br2 or ICl across double bonds; basis of iodine value.","Oxidation: cold dilute KMnO4 gives dihydroxy acids (oleic -> 9,10-dihydroxystearic acid); hot KMnO4 or ozonolysis cleaves at the double bond (oleic acid gives pelargonic and azelaic acids); autoxidation in air causes rancidity.","Isomerisation: nitrous acid converts cis-oleic acid into trans-elaidic acid (elaidinisation).","Drying oils - nature: oils rich in polyunsaturated acids (linolenic, linoleic), e.g. linseed and tung oil, with iodine value above 130; semi-drying oils (soybean, cottonseed) 100-130; non-drying oils (olive, groundnut, castor) below 100.","Drying oils - mechanism and uses: in a thin layer they absorb oxygen; hydroperoxides form at the activated CH2 between double bonds; radicals cross-link the chains into a hard elastic film. Driers (cobalt, manganese, lead salts) catalyse it. Used in paints, varnishes, printing ink and linoleum. Boiled linseed oil dries faster."],"diagram":"","conclusion":"Hydrolysis and saponification break fats into glycerol and fatty acids (soap), fatty acids react at both their COOH group and double bonds, and highly unsaturated drying oils polymerise in air to form protective films."}},{"id":"301L14","unit":3,"m":10,"q":"Explain the principle involved in the determination and the significance of ester value, acetyl value and Reichert-Meissl value.","hi":"Ester = SV-AV; acetyl: acetic anhydride से acetylate; RM: 5 g, steam distil, 0.1 N","kp":[{"t":"Ester value: mg KOH to saponify esters in 1 g; = SV - AV","k":["sv av","ester value","minus"]},{"t":"Ester value shows amount of glycerides/esterified acids","k":["glyceride","esterified"]},{"t":"Acetyl value: acetylation with acetic anhydride","k":["acetic anhydride","acetylat"]},{"t":"Saponify acetylated fat; acetic acid neutralised (mg KOH per 1 g)","k":["acetic acid","acetylated"]},{"t":"Acetyl value measures free OH groups; high in castor oil","k":["hydroxyl","castor","ricinoleic"]},{"t":"RM value: 5 g fat, ml 0.1 N alkali, volatile water-soluble acids","k":["5 g","0.1 n","volatile"]},{"t":"Saponify, acidify, distil under set conditions, titrate distillate","k":["distil","acidif"]},{"t":"RM high for butter/ghee; detects adulteration; Polenske value","k":["butter","ghee","adulterat","polenske"]}],"a":{"intro":"Analytical constants are fixed numbers characteristic of each pure fat or oil. Ester value, acetyl value and Reichert-Meissl value each measure a particular chemical feature: combined fatty acids, free hydroxyl groups and short-chain volatile acids respectively.","points":["Ester value - definition: mg of KOH required to saponify the esters present in 1 g of fat or oil.","Ester value - principle: it is not titrated separately; the acid value (free acids) and the saponification value (free + combined acids) are determined, and Ester value = SV - AV.","Ester value - significance: indicates the proportion of fatty acids present as glycerides; useful for waxes and balsams and in calculating glycerol content of a fat.","Acetyl value - definition: mg of KOH required to neutralise the acetic acid liberated on saponification of 1 g of acetylated fat or oil.","Acetyl value - principle: the fat is boiled with acetic anhydride so that every free -OH group becomes -OCOCH3. Excess anhydride is removed by washing with hot water and the product is dried. The saponification values of the acetylated and the original fat are determined; the increase is due to the acetic acid attached to OH groups, from which the acetyl value is calculated.","Acetyl value - significance: measures free hydroxyl groups (hydroxy fatty acids, mono- and diglycerides, free alcohols such as sterols); castor oil has a high value due to ricinoleic acid, so it identifies castor oil and detects its adulteration.","RM value - definition: ml of 0.1 N aqueous alkali required to neutralise the volatile water-soluble fatty acids distilled from 5 g of fat under specified conditions.","RM value - principle: 5 g fat is saponified with glycerol-NaOH, diluted with water, acidified with dilute H2SO4 and distilled with pumice at a fixed rate to a fixed volume. Volatile water-soluble acids (butyric, caproic) collect in the distillate; insoluble acids are filtered off; the filtrate is titrated with 0.1 N NaOH (phenolphthalein); blank correction is applied.","RM value - significance: butter fat and ghee give high values due to butyric acid; most vegetable oils and animal body fats give very low values; so it detects adulteration of ghee/butter with vanaspati or animal fats. The insoluble volatile acids washed from the condenser give the Polenske value (high for coconut oil)."],"diagram":"Draw the RM distillation set-up: a flat-bottom/round-bottom flask containing the acidified soap solution and pumice, heated by a burner, connected through a bulb tube to a water-cooled condenser delivering into a graduated receiving flask (fixed volume mark).","conclusion":"Ester value shows combined acids, acetyl value shows free hydroxyl groups and RM value shows short-chain volatile acids; together with other constants they identify oils and detect adulteration, especially of ghee and castor oil."}},{"id":"301L15","unit":4,"m":10,"q":"Define polynuclear hydrocarbons. Explain the Haworth synthesis, structure, reactions and medicinal uses of naphthalene and anthracene.","hi":"Haworth: benzene + succinic anhydride → ... → naphthalene; phthalic anhydride → anthraquinone → anthracene","kp":[{"t":"Definition and classification: isolated vs fused rings","k":["fused","isolated","two or more"]},{"t":"Haworth naphthalene: benzene + succinic anhydride (AlCl3)","k":["succinic anhydride","haworth"]},{"t":"Clemmensen reduction, cyclisation (H2SO4), dehydrogenation (Se/Pd) via tetralone/tetralin","k":["clemmensen","tetralone","tetralin","selenium"]},{"t":"Naphthalene structure: C10H8, 10 pi electrons, alpha and beta positions","k":["c10h8","10 pi","alpha","beta"]},{"t":"Naphthalene reactions: 1-substitution, oxidation to phthalic anhydride, reduction to tetralin/decalin","k":["phthalic anhydride","decalin","nitronaphthalene"]},{"t":"Anthracene Haworth: benzene + phthalic anhydride gives o-benzoylbenzoic acid then anthraquinone","k":["phthalic anhydride","benzoylbenzoic","anthraquinone"]},{"t":"Anthracene: C14H10, linear, reactive 9,10 positions","k":["c14h10","9 10","linear"]},{"t":"Anthracene reactions: oxidation to anthraquinone, Diels-Alder, 9,10-dihydro","k":["diels","dihydroanthracene","maleic"]},{"t":"Uses: naproxen, propranolol, mothballs; anthraquinone laxatives, dithranol","k":["naproxen","propranolol","moth","laxative","dithranol"]}],"a":{"intro":"Polynuclear hydrocarbons are aromatic hydrocarbons with two or more benzene rings, either isolated (biphenyl, diphenylmethane, triphenylmethane) or fused, sharing two carbons (naphthalene, anthracene, phenanthrene). Naphthalene (C10H8) has two and anthracene (C14H10) three linearly fused rings.","points":["Haworth synthesis of naphthalene: (1) benzene + succinic anhydride (AlCl3, Friedel-Crafts acylation) -> beta-benzoylpropionic acid; (2) Clemmensen reduction (Zn-Hg/HCl) -> gamma-phenylbutyric acid; (3) conc. H2SO4 (intramolecular acylation) -> alpha-tetralone; (4) Clemmensen reduction -> tetralin; (5) dehydrogenation with Se or Pd on heating -> naphthalene.","Other source: naphthalene is obtained commercially from the middle and heavy oil fractions of coal tar.","Structure of naphthalene: planar, two fused rings, 10 pi electrons (4n+2, n = 2), resonance energy about 61 kcal/mol (less than two benzenes); C1-C2 bonds are shorter than C2-C3 bonds; positions 1,4,5,8 are alpha and 2,3,6,7 are beta.","Reactions of naphthalene: electrophilic substitution at C1 (nitration -> 1-nitronaphthalene; halogenation -> 1-bromonaphthalene; sulphonation -> 1-sulphonic acid at 80 C, 2-sulphonic acid at 160 C); oxidation (V2O5/air -> phthalic anhydride; CrO3 -> 1,4-naphthoquinone); reduction (Na/ethanol -> 1,4-dihydronaphthalene; Na/isoamyl alcohol -> tetralin; H2/Ni -> decalin).","Uses of naphthalene: moth repellent, manufacture of phthalic anhydride, naphthols and dyes; drugs with naphthalene ring - naproxen, propranolol, naphazoline, tolnaftate, nafcillin, menadione (vitamin K3).","Haworth synthesis of anthracene: benzene + phthalic anhydride (AlCl3) -> o-benzoylbenzoic acid; conc. H2SO4 cyclises it to 9,10-anthraquinone; distillation with zinc dust (reduction) -> anthracene.","Other syntheses of anthracene: Friedel-Crafts condensation of two molecules of benzyl chloride (AlCl3) gives 9,10-dihydroanthracene, which is dehydrogenated; Elbs reaction (pyrolysis of o-methylbenzophenone); also obtained from the anthracene oil fraction of coal tar.","Structure and reactions of anthracene: planar, 14 pi electrons; positions 9 and 10 are most reactive because attack there leaves two benzene rings intact. Oxidation (Na2Cr2O7/H2SO4) -> 9,10-anthraquinone; reduction (Na/ethanol) -> 9,10-dihydroanthracene; Diels-Alder with maleic anhydride at 9,10; Br2 adds at 9,10 then loses HBr to give 9-bromoanthracene.","Uses of anthracene: manufacture of anthraquinone and alizarin dyes; anthraquinone nucleus in senna/cascara/aloe laxative glycosides, dithranol (psoriasis) and anthracycline anticancer drugs (doxorubicin)."],"diagram":"Draw the Haworth sequence as a flow chart with structures: benzene + succinic anhydride -> beta-benzoylpropionic acid -> gamma-phenylbutyric acid -> alpha-tetralone -> tetralin -> naphthalene; and benzene + phthalic anhydride -> o-benzoylbenzoic acid -> anthraquinone -> anthracene. Number naphthalene (1-8) and anthracene (1-10, with 9,10 in the middle ring).","conclusion":"The Haworth method builds new rings by Friedel-Crafts acylation, reduction, cyclisation and dehydrogenation; naphthalene reacts at C1 and anthracene at C9/C10, and both supply nuclei for many drugs."}},{"id":"301L16","unit":4,"m":10,"q":"Give the synthesis and medicinal uses of naphthalene, phenanthrene and anthracene. Add a note on the structure and reactions of phenanthrene.","hi":"Phenanthrene: angular, 9,10 bond reactive; Haworth (naphthalene + succinic anhydride), Pschorr","kp":[{"t":"Naphthalene: Haworth or Fittig (phenylisocrotonic acid to alpha-naphthol)","k":["haworth","fittig","phenylisocrotonic","alpha naphthol"]},{"t":"Anthracene: phthalic anhydride route / Elbs reaction","k":["phthalic anhydride","elbs","anthraquinone"]},{"t":"Phenanthrene Haworth: naphthalene + succinic anhydride","k":["naphthalene succinic","succinic anhydride"]},{"t":"Pschorr synthesis (diazotisation, Cu cyclisation)","k":["pschorr","copper","diazoti"]},{"t":"Phenanthrene: angular fusion, 9,10 bond like a double bond","k":["angular","9 10"]},{"t":"Phenanthrene reactions: oxidation to 9,10-phenanthraquinone, Br2 addition, H2 to 9,10-dihydro","k":["phenanthraquinone","dihydrophenanthrene","dibromo"]},{"t":"Medicinal: steroid nucleus, morphine/codeine","k":["steroid","morphine","cyclopentanoperhydrophenanthrene"]},{"t":"Naphthalene/anthracene drugs: naproxen, propranolol; laxatives, dithranol","k":["naproxen","propranolol","laxative","dithranol"]}],"a":{"intro":"Naphthalene (C10H8), anthracene (C14H10, linear) and phenanthrene (C14H10, angular) are fused polynuclear hydrocarbons. All occur in coal tar, can be synthesised in the laboratory and form the skeletons of many drugs.","points":["Naphthalene synthesis 1 (Haworth): benzene + succinic anhydride/AlCl3 -> beta-benzoylpropionic acid -> Clemmensen -> gamma-phenylbutyric acid -> H2SO4 -> alpha-tetralone -> reduction -> tetralin -> Se/heat -> naphthalene.","Naphthalene synthesis 2 (Fittig): beta-benzylidenepropionic acid (phenylisocrotonic acid) cyclises on heating to alpha-naphthol, which on distillation with zinc dust gives naphthalene.","Anthracene synthesis: (a) benzene + phthalic anhydride/AlCl3 -> o-benzoylbenzoic acid -> conc. H2SO4 -> anthraquinone -> zinc dust distillation -> anthracene; (b) Elbs reaction: pyrolysis of o-methylbenzophenone; (c) self-condensation of benzyl chloride with AlCl3 then dehydrogenation.","Phenanthrene synthesis (Haworth): naphthalene + succinic anhydride (AlCl3, nitrobenzene) -> naphthoylpropionic acid -> Clemmensen -> naphthylbutyric acid -> H2SO4 cyclisation -> cyclic ketone -> Clemmensen -> tetrahydrophenanthrene -> Se dehydrogenation -> phenanthrene.","Phenanthrene synthesis (Pschorr): o-nitrobenzaldehyde + sodium phenylacetate (Perkin condensation) -> alpha-phenyl-o-nitrocinnamic acid; reduction of NO2 to NH2, diazotisation and Cu powder cyclisation -> phenanthrene-9-carboxylic acid -> decarboxylation -> phenanthrene.","Structure of phenanthrene: three rings fused angularly, 14 pi electrons, planar; resonance energy greater than anthracene, so it is more stable. The C9-C10 bond has high double-bond character.","Reactions of phenanthrene: occur mainly at 9,10. Oxidation (CrO3 or K2Cr2O7/H2SO4) -> 9,10-phenanthraquinone (further -> diphenic acid); hydrogenation (H2/Pt or Na/alcohol) -> 9,10-dihydrophenanthrene; Br2 adds to give 9,10-dibromo-9,10-dihydrophenanthrene, which loses HBr on heating to 9-bromophenanthrene; nitration and sulphonation give mixtures.","Medicinal uses - naphthalene: naproxen, propranolol, naphazoline, tolnaftate, menadione; moth repellent.","Medicinal uses - anthracene: anthraquinone laxatives (senna, cascara, aloe), dithranol for psoriasis, anthracycline anticancer antibiotics.","Medicinal uses - phenanthrene: perhydro form in the steroid (cyclopentanoperhydrophenanthrene) nucleus of cholesterol, sex hormones, corticosteroids, cardiac glycosides and vitamin D; partially reduced form in morphine and codeine."],"diagram":"Draw naphthalene, anthracene (three rings in a straight line) and phenanthrene (three rings in an angular, bent arrangement) with numbering; mark the reactive 9,10 positions of anthracene and phenanthrene; draw the steroid nucleus (rings A, B, C six-membered and ring D five-membered) to show the phenanthrene part.","conclusion":"All three hydrocarbons can be built by Friedel-Crafts based ring-closing methods; phenanthrene reacts mainly at its 9,10 double bond and, like naphthalene and anthracene, provides the skeleton of important drugs."}},{"id":"301L17","unit":4,"m":10,"q":"Describe the methods of preparation, reactions and medicinal uses of diphenylmethane and triphenylmethane and their derivatives.","hi":"Friedel-Crafts से दोनों; benzhydryl antihistamines; triphenylmethane dyes, trityl","kp":[{"t":"Diphenylmethane: benzene + benzyl chloride (AlCl3)","k":["benzyl chloride","c6h5ch2cl","friedel"]},{"t":"Diphenylmethane: benzene + CH2Cl2 or HCHO; reduction of benzophenone","k":["ch2cl2","dichloromethane","benzophenone","formaldehyde"]},{"t":"Diphenylmethane oxidation gives benzophenone; CH2 is reactive","k":["benzophenone","oxidat"]},{"t":"Benzhydryl drugs: diphenhydramine, cyclizine, cetirizine","k":["diphenhydramine","cyclizine","cetirizine","benzhydryl"]},{"t":"Triphenylmethane: benzene + CHCl3 (AlCl3)","k":["chloroform","chcl3"]},{"t":"Trityl cation/radical/anion stable by resonance","k":["trityl","triphenylmethyl","stable"]},{"t":"Triphenylcarbinol from oxidation","k":["triphenylcarbinol","triphenylmethanol"]},{"t":"Dyes: crystal violet, malachite green, phenolphthalein; clotrimazole","k":["crystal violet","gentian","malachite","phenolphthalein","clotrimazole"]}],"a":{"intro":"Diphenylmethane, (C6H5)2CH2, and triphenylmethane, (C6H5)3CH, are polynuclear hydrocarbons with isolated (non-fused) benzene rings attached to one central carbon. Their derivatives include antihistamines, dyes, antiseptics and indicators.","points":["Diphenylmethane - preparation: (a) Friedel-Crafts: C6H6 + C6H5CH2Cl --AlCl3--> (C6H5)2CH2 + HCl; (b) 2C6H6 + CH2Cl2 (AlCl3); (c) benzene + formaldehyde with conc. H2SO4; (d) reduction of benzophenone (Clemmensen or HI/P).","Diphenylmethane - properties and reactions: colourless crystalline solid with orange odour; the CH2 hydrogens are activated by two rings; oxidation (CrO3) gives benzophenone; bromination of CH2 in light gives benzhydryl bromide; ring substitution (nitration) occurs at para positions.","Diphenylmethane - uses and derivatives: perfumery; benzhydryl (diphenylmethyl) group in H1-antihistamines diphenhydramine and orphenadrine (ethers), cyclizine, meclizine, hydroxyzine and cetirizine (piperazines), and in benztropine (anticholinergic, Parkinsonism). DDT is a related diphenylethane.","Triphenylmethane - preparation: (a) Friedel-Crafts: 3C6H6 + CHCl3 --AlCl3--> (C6H5)3CH + 3HCl; (b) benzene + CCl4/AlCl3 -> triphenylmethyl chloride, then reduction; (c) benzal chloride + benzene (AlCl3).","Triphenylmethane - reactions: oxidation (CrO3) gives triphenylcarbinol; triphenylcarbinol + HCl gives trityl chloride; the central carbon readily forms the triphenylmethyl cation (coloured, stable), free radical and anion (with strong bases), all stabilised by delocalisation over three rings; trityl chloride protects primary OH groups (trityl ethers) in synthesis.","Triphenylmethane dyes: amino or hydroxy groups at para positions give intensely coloured dyes - malachite green, crystal violet (gentian violet, antiseptic/antifungal and Gram stain), brilliant green (antiseptic), rosaniline/basic fuchsin (used in Ziehl-Neelsen staining).","Other derivatives: phenolphthalein (acid-base indicator, colourless in acid, pink in alkali; formerly a laxative); bromophenol blue and bromocresol green indicators; clotrimazole (antifungal) contains a trityl group linked to imidazole."],"diagram":"Draw diphenylmethane (two benzene rings joined by CH2) and triphenylmethane (three benzene rings on one CH); draw the trityl cation showing the + charge delocalised into the three rings; draw diphenhydramine as (C6H5)2CH-O-CH2CH2-N(CH3)2.","conclusion":"Both hydrocarbons are made by Friedel-Crafts reactions; the diphenylmethyl group is the core of many antihistamines, and triphenylmethane gives dyes, antiseptics, indicators and the antifungal clotrimazole."}},{"id":"301L18","unit":5,"m":10,"q":"Explain Baeyer's strain theory. Calculate the angle strain in different cycloalkanes and discuss the limitations of the theory.","hi":"Baeyer: ring planar, angle deviation = strain; cyclopropane 24°44′; cyclohexane गलत predict","kp":[{"t":"Rings assumed planar; C-C-C angles equal to regular polygon angles","k":["planar","regular polygon"]},{"t":"Deviation from tetrahedral 109 28' causes angle strain","k":["109 28","109.5","tetrahedral","deviation"]},{"t":"Formula d = 1/2 (109 28' - ring angle)","k":["1 2","half","formula"]},{"t":"Values: cyclopropane 24 44', cyclobutane 9 44', cyclopentane 0 44', cyclohexane -5 16'","k":["24 44","9 44","0 44","5 16"]},{"t":"Predicted cyclopentane most stable, explains reactivity of 3/4 rings","k":["cyclopentane most stable","reactivity"]},{"t":"Limitation: rings larger than C5 are puckered, not planar","k":["puckered","non planar"]},{"t":"Limitation: cyclohexane and large rings are strain-free (heat of combustion 157.4)","k":["157.4","heat of combustion","strain free"]},{"t":"Modified by Sachse-Mohr and Coulson-Moffitt","k":["sachse","mohr","coulson"]}],"a":{"intro":"Baeyer's strain theory explains the relative stability of cycloalkanes in terms of bond-angle strain. It assumes that all ring carbons lie in one plane, so the C-C-C angles are those of a regular polygon.","points":["Postulate 1: all ring carbon atoms lie in one plane (planar rings).","Postulate 2: the normal (strain-free) valence angle of carbon is the tetrahedral angle 109 deg 28'; any deviation of the ring angle from it produces angle strain (Baeyer strain), making the ring unstable.","Postulate 3: the greater the deviation, the greater the strain and the lower the stability (and higher reactivity).","Calculation: deviation per bond d = 1/2 (109 deg 28' - internal angle of planar ring). The factor 1/2 is used because the distortion is shared by both bonds forming the angle.","Values: cyclopropane (60 deg) +24 deg 44'; cyclobutane (90 deg) +9 deg 44'; cyclopentane (108 deg) +0 deg 44'; cyclohexane (120 deg) -5 deg 16'; cycloheptane (128 deg 34') -9 deg 33'; cyclooctane (135 deg) -12 deg 46'.","Conclusions of Baeyer: cyclopropane is most strained and most reactive (ring-opening with H2, Br2, HBr), cyclobutane less so; cyclopentane should be the most stable; cyclohexane and larger rings should be increasingly strained (negative deviation).","Successes: explains why cyclopropane and cyclobutane undergo ring-opening addition reactions and why 5- and 6-membered rings form most easily.","Limitation 1: rings are not planar. Cyclohexane and larger rings pucker so that normal tetrahedral angles are kept.","Limitation 2: heats of combustion per CH2 (cyclopropane 166.6, cyclobutane 164.0, cyclopentane 158.7, cyclohexane 157.4 kcal/mol; open chain 157.4) show cyclohexane is strain-free and more stable than cyclopentane, contrary to Baeyer.","Limitation 3: large rings (C15-C30, e.g. musk ketones) are stable and are found in nature; Baeyer's theory cannot explain this. It also ignores torsional (eclipsing) strain and the bent bonds of small rings (explained by Coulson and Moffitt)."],"diagram":"Draw planar polygons for cyclopropane (triangle, 60 deg), cyclobutane (square, 90 deg), cyclopentane (pentagon, 108 deg) and cyclohexane (hexagon, 120 deg) and mark the angle compared with 109 deg 28'. Tabulate ring size, ring angle and deviation.","conclusion":"Baeyer's theory correctly explains the instability of three- and four-membered rings, but it fails for six-membered and larger rings because these are puckered and strain-free, as shown by Sachse-Mohr theory and heats of combustion."}},{"id":"301L19","unit":5,"m":10,"q":"Discuss the Sachse-Mohr theory of strainless rings and the Coulson-Moffitt modification of Baeyer's strain theory. Explain the conformations of cyclohexane.","hi":"Sachse-Mohr: puckered chair/boat strainless; Coulson-Moffitt: banana bonds","kp":[{"t":"Sachse: cyclohexane and larger rings are puckered (non-planar)","k":["puckered","non planar"]},{"t":"Normal tetrahedral angle retained, so strainless","k":["109","tetrahedral","strainless","strain free"]},{"t":"Two forms: chair and boat","k":["chair","boat"]},{"t":"Mohr: chair and boat interconvert; decalin cis/trans predicted","k":["decalin","cis","trans"]},{"t":"Chair more stable: staggered, no flagpole interaction","k":["staggered","flagpole","eclipsed"]},{"t":"Axial and equatorial bonds; bulky groups equatorial","k":["axial","equatorial"]},{"t":"Coulson-Moffitt: bent (banana) bonds in cyclopropane","k":["bent bond","banana"]},{"t":"Interorbital angle about 104; poor overlap causes strain","k":["104","overlap"]}],"a":{"intro":"Baeyer's theory failed for six-membered and larger rings. Sachse and Mohr explained that such rings are puckered and strain-free (theory of strainless rings), and Coulson and Moffitt explained the true nature of strain in small rings using orbital overlap.","points":["Sachse's proposal: cyclohexane is not planar; its carbons lie in two parallel planes so that every C-C-C angle is the tetrahedral 109.5 degrees. Hence the ring is free of angle strain.","Two strainless forms: the chair form and the boat form. Sachse expected them to be separable, but they could not be isolated.","Mohr's extension: the chair and boat forms interconvert rapidly by rotation about C-C bonds, so they cannot be separated; the chair is the stable form. Mohr predicted that decalin should exist as two stable isomers (cis and trans), which were later isolated, confirming the theory.","Chair conformation: all adjacent C-H bonds staggered; no angle or torsional strain; the most stable conformation.","Boat conformation: eclipsed C-H bonds along the sides (torsional strain) and repulsion between the two flagpole hydrogens; about 7 kcal/mol less stable than chair. Twist-boat lies in between.","Axial and equatorial bonds: in the chair, six bonds are axial (parallel to the ring axis) and six equatorial (around the ring). Ring flip interconverts them; bulky substituents prefer equatorial positions to avoid 1,3-diaxial interactions.","Larger rings: also puckered and essentially strain-free; heats of combustion per CH2 are close to 157.4 kcal/mol.","Coulson-Moffitt modification: in cyclopropane the carbon orbitals cannot be bent to 60 degrees; the C-C bonding orbitals have more p character and an interorbital angle of about 104 degrees, so they overlap outside the C-C axis, forming bent or banana bonds.","Consequence: bent bonds overlap less effectively and are weaker, giving strain energy and alkene-like reactivity (ring opening with H2, Br2, HBr). Strain therefore means poor overlap, not a compressed bond angle. Cyclobutane is also slightly puckered to reduce eclipsing."],"diagram":"Draw cyclohexane in the chair form with 6 axial (vertical) and 6 equatorial (outward) bonds, and in the boat form with the two flagpole hydrogens facing each other. Draw cyclopropane as a triangle with curved (banana) bond lines bulging outward from the C-C lines, marking the 104 degree interorbital angle.","conclusion":"Sachse-Mohr theory shows six-membered and larger rings are puckered and strainless (chair form most stable), while the Coulson-Moffitt bent-bond model explains why small rings are strained; together they correct Baeyer's strain theory."}},{"id":"301L20","unit":5,"m":10,"q":"Give the general methods of preparation of cycloalkanes and the reactions of cyclopropane and cyclobutane.","hi":"Freund, dicarboxylic salts, malonic ester, Simmons-Smith; cyclopropane ring opening","kp":[{"t":"Freund's method: dihalide + Zn/Na (intramolecular Wurtz)","k":["freund","wurtz","dibromopropane"]},{"t":"Heating Ca/Ba salts of dicarboxylic acids gives cyclic ketone, then Clemmensen","k":["calcium","adipate","dicarboxylic","cyclopentanone"]},{"t":"Malonic ester synthesis with dihalide","k":["malonic","malonate"]},{"t":"Simmons-Smith / carbene addition to alkenes","k":["simmons","carbene","ch2i2"]},{"t":"Hydrogenation of benzene gives cyclohexane","k":["hydrogenation of benzene","benzene"]},{"t":"Cyclopropane + H2/Ni (80 C) propane; cyclobutane (200 C) butane","k":["80","200","propane","butane"]},{"t":"Cyclopropane + Br2 gives 1,3-dibromopropane; + HBr gives 1-bromopropane","k":["dibromopropane","bromopropane","hbr"]},{"t":"Cyclopropane + H2SO4 gives n-propyl alcohol after hydrolysis","k":["h2so4","propyl alcohol","propanol"]},{"t":"Cyclobutane gives substitution with halogens; not with HBr","k":["substitution","bromocyclobutane","chlorocyclobutane"]}],"a":{"intro":"Cycloalkanes are saturated cyclic hydrocarbons (CnH2n). Small rings (cyclopropane, cyclobutane) are strained and undergo ring-opening reactions like alkenes, while larger rings behave like alkanes.","points":["Freund's method (intramolecular Wurtz): alpha,omega-dihalides with Zn or Na: BrCH2CH2CH2Br + Zn -> cyclopropane; 1,4-dibromobutane + Na -> cyclobutane.","From dicarboxylic acid salts: dry distillation of calcium or barium salts gives cyclic ketones (calcium adipate -> cyclopentanone; calcium pimelate -> cyclohexanone), which are reduced to cycloalkanes by Clemmensen (Zn-Hg/HCl) or Wolff-Kishner reduction.","Malonic ester synthesis: diethyl malonate + 1,2-dibromoethane + 2 NaOEt -> diethyl cyclopropane-1,1-dicarboxylate; hydrolysis and decarboxylation give cyclopropanecarboxylic acid (1,3-dibromopropane similarly gives cyclobutane derivatives).","Simmons-Smith and carbene addition: an alkene + CH2I2 + Zn(Cu), or diazomethane in light, adds CH2 across C=C to give cyclopropanes.","Other methods: catalytic hydrogenation of benzene (Ni, heat, pressure) gives cyclohexane; Diels-Alder reaction followed by hydrogenation gives cyclohexane derivatives; Dieckmann condensation gives cyclic keto esters.","Cyclopropane - hydrogenation: H2/Ni at about 80 C opens the ring to propane.","Cyclopropane - halogens and halogen acids: Br2 (or Cl2/FeCl3) adds with ring opening to give 1,3-dihalopropane; Cl2 in sunlight gives substitution (chlorocyclopropane); HBr or HI gives 1-bromo- or 1-iodopropane.","Cyclopropane - sulphuric acid: conc. H2SO4 opens the ring to n-propyl hydrogen sulphate, which on hydrolysis gives n-propyl alcohol. Unlike propene, cyclopropane does not decolourise cold dilute KMnO4 (distinguishing test). It burns and was formerly used as a general anaesthetic gas.","Cyclobutane - reactions: H2/Ni needs about 200 C to give n-butane; with Cl2 or Br2 in light it gives substitution (chloro/bromocyclobutane) without ring opening; it does not react with HBr or H2SO4 under ordinary conditions.","Reactivity order: cyclopropane > cyclobutane > cyclopentane, in line with ring strain."],"diagram":"Draw a reaction chart: cyclopropane in the centre with arrows to propane (H2/Ni, 80 C), 1,3-dibromopropane (Br2), 1-bromopropane (HBr), n-propyl hydrogen sulphate (H2SO4) and chlorocyclopropane (Cl2, light); beside it cyclobutane -> n-butane (H2/Ni, 200 C) and bromocyclobutane (Br2, light).","conclusion":"Cycloalkanes are prepared mainly by ring-closing reactions of dihalides, dicarboxylic acid salts or malonic ester and by additions to alkenes; cyclopropane readily opens like an alkene, cyclobutane less readily, reflecting their ring strain."}}],"mcq":[{"id":"301M01","unit":1,"q":"The resonance energy of benzene obtained from heats of hydrogenation is about:","options":["36 kcal/mol","28.6 kcal/mol","49.8 kcal/mol","85.8 kcal/mol"],"answer":0,"explain":"Expected 85.8 (3 x 28.6) minus observed 49.8 = about 36 kcal/mol."},{"id":"301M02","unit":1,"q":"Which of the following is NOT aromatic?","options":["Tropylium cation","Cyclooctatetraene","Cyclopentadienyl anion","Pyridine"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cyclooctatetraene has 8 pi electrons and is tub-shaped (non-planar), so it is non-aromatic."},{"id":"301M03","unit":1,"q":"In nitration of benzene, the actual electrophile is:","options":["NO2-","NO+","NO2+","HNO3"],"answer":2,"explain":"Conc. H2SO4 protonates HNO3, which loses water to form the nitronium ion NO2+."},{"id":"301M04","unit":1,"q":"Which electrophilic substitution of benzene is reversible?","options":["Nitration","Chlorination","Friedel-Crafts acylation","Sulphonation"],"answer":3,"explain":"Benzenesulphonic acid loses SO3H on heating with dilute acid or steam."},{"id":"301M05","unit":1,"q":"Friedel-Crafts alkylation of benzene with n-propyl chloride/AlCl3 gives mainly:","options":["Isopropylbenzene","n-Propylbenzene","Propiophenone","1,3-Dipropylbenzene"],"answer":0,"explain":"The primary carbocation rearranges by hydride shift to the secondary isopropyl cation."},{"id":"301M06","unit":1,"q":"Which compound does NOT undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction?","options":["Toluene","Nitrobenzene","Anisole","Benzene"],"answer":1,"explain":"-NO2 strongly deactivates the ring; nitrobenzene is even used as a solvent for FC reactions."},{"id":"301M07","unit":1,"q":"Chlorine on benzene ring is:","options":["Activating and meta directing","Activating and ortho/para directing","Deactivating and ortho/para directing","Deactivating and meta directing"],"answer":2,"explain":"-I effect deactivates; +R effect of lone pairs directs ortho/para."},{"id":"301M08","unit":1,"q":"Gammexane (lindane) is:","options":["p,p'-DDT","N-chloro-p-toluenesulphonamide","o-sulphobenzimide","gamma isomer of hexachlorocyclohexane"],"answer":3,"explain":"Gamma-BHC (C6H6Cl6) is the active insecticidal isomer, used for scabies and lice."},{"id":"301M09","unit":1,"q":"Saccharin is chemically:","options":["o-Sulphobenzimide","p-Toluenesulphonamide","Benzoic sulphonyl chloride","Sodium N-chlorobenzenesulphonamide"],"answer":0,"explain":"Saccharin = o-sulphobenzimide, prepared from toluene."},{"id":"301M10","unit":1,"q":"Which of the following groups is the strongest activator of the benzene ring?","options":["-CH3","-O- (phenoxide)","-NHCOCH3","-Cl"],"answer":1,"explain":"Phenoxide oxygen carries a full negative charge and donates electrons most strongly."},{"id":"301M11","unit":1,"q":"Number of pi electrons in the cyclopentadienyl anion and its nature is:","options":["4, antiaromatic","5, non-aromatic","6, aromatic","6, non-aromatic"],"answer":2,"explain":"Planar, cyclic, conjugated with 6 pi electrons (n = 1)."},{"id":"301M12","unit":2,"q":"Which is the strongest acid?","options":["Phenol","p-Cresol","m-Nitrophenol","p-Nitrophenol"],"answer":3,"explain":"-NO2 at para stabilises phenoxide by resonance and induction; at meta only by induction."},{"id":"301M13","unit":2,"q":"Phenol on treatment with CHCl3 and aqueous NaOH followed by acidification gives:","options":["Salicylaldehyde","Salicylic acid","Benzaldehyde","Phenyl chloroformate"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reimer-Tiemann reaction; the electrophile is dichlorocarbene."},{"id":"301M14","unit":2,"q":"The electrophile in Reimer-Tiemann reaction is:","options":["CHCl2+",":CCl2","CCl3+","Cl+"],"answer":1,"explain":"CHCl3 + OH- gives CCl3-, which loses Cl- to give dichlorocarbene."},{"id":"301M15","unit":2,"q":"Allyl phenyl ether on heating to about 200 C gives o-allylphenol. This is:","options":["Fries rearrangement","Beckmann rearrangement","Claisen rearrangement","Hofmann rearrangement"],"answer":2,"explain":"An intramolecular [3,3]-sigmatropic shift."},{"id":"301M16","unit":2,"q":"Arrange in decreasing order of basicity: (I) aniline, (II) p-toluidine, (III) p-nitroaniline, (IV) methylamine","options":["II > IV > I > III","IV > I > II > III","I > II > IV > III","IV > II > I > III"],"answer":3,"explain":"Alkylamine most basic; EDG at para raises and NO2 lowers aniline's basicity."},{"id":"301M17","unit":2,"q":"Benzenediazonium chloride on warming with CuCN/KCN gives:","options":["Benzonitrile","Phenyl isocyanide","Benzamide","Aniline"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sandmeyer reaction replaces N2+ by CN."},{"id":"301M18","unit":2,"q":"Fluorobenzene is best prepared from benzenediazonium salt by:","options":["Sandmeyer reaction","Balz-Schiemann reaction","Gattermann reaction","Heating with KF"],"answer":1,"explain":"The diazonium fluoroborate (from HBF4) decomposes on heating to ArF."},{"id":"301M19","unit":2,"q":"Which acid is the strongest?","options":["Benzoic acid","p-Toluic acid","o-Nitrobenzoic acid","p-Methoxybenzoic acid"],"answer":2,"explain":"Nitro is electron-withdrawing and the ortho effect further increases acidity."},{"id":"301M20","unit":2,"q":"The reagent of Kolbe-Schmitt reaction is:","options":["Phenol + CCl4 + NaOH","Phenol + CO + HCl","Phenol + HCHO","Sodium phenoxide + CO2 under pressure"],"answer":3,"explain":"Sodium phenoxide + CO2 (about 125 C, 4-7 atm) gives sodium salicylate."},{"id":"301M21","unit":2,"q":"Which test is given by phenol with NaNO2 and conc. H2SO4 (green/blue colour, red on dilution)?","options":["Liebermann's nitroso test","Phthalein test","Carbylamine test","Molisch test"],"answer":0,"explain":"Liebermann's test for phenols."},{"id":"301M22","unit":2,"q":"Coupling of benzenediazonium chloride with phenol is carried out in:","options":["Strongly acidic medium","Mildly alkaline medium","Neutral boiling water","Concentrated H2SO4"],"answer":1,"explain":"Mild alkali forms the reactive phenoxide; strong alkali converts diazonium to diazotate."},{"id":"301M23","unit":3,"q":"Reichert-Meissl value is defined for how much fat?","options":["1 g","10 g","5 g","100 g"],"answer":2,"explain":"RM value: ml of 0.1 N alkali for volatile water-soluble acids from 5 g fat."},{"id":"301M24","unit":3,"q":"Iodine value of a drying oil is:","options":["Below 100","100-130","Exactly zero","Above 130"],"answer":3,"explain":"Drying oils such as linseed oil are highly unsaturated, IV above 130."},{"id":"301M25","unit":3,"q":"Wijs reagent used in iodine value determination is:","options":["Iodine monochloride in glacial acetic acid","Iodine in KI","Iodine monobromide in acetic acid","Iodine in ethanol"],"answer":0,"explain":"Wijs solution = ICl in glacial acetic acid (Hanus uses IBr)."},{"id":"301M26","unit":3,"q":"A fat with high saponification value contains mainly:","options":["Long-chain fatty acids","Short-chain fatty acids","Hydroxy fatty acids","Unsaponifiable matter"],"answer":1,"explain":"SV is inversely proportional to mean molecular weight."},{"id":"301M27","unit":3,"q":"High acetyl value is characteristic of:","options":["Coconut oil","Linseed oil","Castor oil","Olive oil"],"answer":2,"explain":"Ricinoleic acid in castor oil has a free OH group."},{"id":"301M28","unit":3,"q":"Ester value is equal to:","options":["Acid value - saponification value","Saponification value + acid value","Iodine value - acid value","Saponification value - acid value"],"answer":3,"explain":"Esters = total saponifiable minus free acids."},{"id":"301M29","unit":3,"q":"Butyric acid odour in stored butter is due to:","options":["Hydrolytic rancidity","Oxidative rancidity","Hydrogenation","Elaidinisation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lipase hydrolyses butter fat releasing volatile butyric acid."},{"id":"301M30","unit":3,"q":"Oleic acid on treatment with nitrous acid gives:","options":["Stearic acid","Elaidic acid","Linoleic acid","9,10-Dihydroxystearic acid"],"answer":1,"explain":"Elaidinisation converts cis-oleic acid into trans-elaidic acid."},{"id":"301M31","unit":3,"q":"Which antioxidant is NOT used to prevent rancidity of oils?","options":["BHA","BHT","Sodium chloride","Tocopherol"],"answer":2,"explain":"BHA, BHT and tocopherol are antioxidants; NaCl is not."},{"id":"301M32","unit":3,"q":"In saponification value determination, excess KOH is back-titrated with:","options":["0.1 N NaOH","0.1 N sodium thiosulphate","0.1 N iodine","0.5 N HCl"],"answer":3,"explain":"Excess 0.5 N alcoholic KOH is titrated with 0.5 N HCl using phenolphthalein."},{"id":"301M33","unit":3,"q":"Which oil has the highest iodine value?","options":["Linseed oil","Coconut oil","Olive oil","Groundnut oil"],"answer":0,"explain":"Linseed oil is rich in linolenic acid, a drying oil (IV above 170)."},{"id":"301M34","unit":4,"q":"Haworth synthesis of naphthalene starts with benzene and:","options":["Phthalic anhydride","Succinic anhydride","Maleic anhydride","Acetic anhydride"],"answer":1,"explain":"Benzene + succinic anhydride (AlCl3) gives beta-benzoylpropionic acid, the first intermediate."},{"id":"301M35","unit":4,"q":"Sulphonation of naphthalene at about 160 C mainly gives:","options":["Naphthalene-1-sulphonic acid","Naphthalene-1,5-disulphonic acid","Naphthalene-2-sulphonic acid","Phthalic acid"],"answer":2,"explain":"High temperature favours the thermodynamically more stable beta (2-) isomer."},{"id":"301M36","unit":4,"q":"Catalytic air oxidation of naphthalene over V2O5 gives:","options":["1,4-Naphthoquinone","Benzoic acid","Maleic anhydride","Phthalic anhydride"],"answer":3,"explain":"One ring is oxidised away leaving phthalic anhydride."},{"id":"301M37","unit":4,"q":"The most reactive positions in anthracene are:","options":["9 and 10","1 and 4","2 and 3","1 and 8"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reaction at 9,10 leaves two intact benzene rings."},{"id":"301M38","unit":4,"q":"Cyclopentanoperhydrophenanthrene nucleus is present in:","options":["Morphine","Cholesterol","Naproxen","Saccharin"],"answer":1,"explain":"It is the steroid nucleus (cholesterol, hormones, cardiac glycosides)."},{"id":"301M39","unit":4,"q":"Gentian (crystal) violet is a derivative of:","options":["Diphenylmethane","Anthracene","Triphenylmethane","Naphthalene"],"answer":2,"explain":"Crystal violet and malachite green are triphenylmethane dyes."},{"id":"301M40","unit":4,"q":"Diphenhydramine contains which nucleus?","options":["Phenanthrene","Naphthalene","Anthracene","Diphenylmethane (benzhydryl)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Diphenhydramine is a benzhydryl ether of dimethylaminoethanol."},{"id":"301M41","unit":4,"q":"Reduction of naphthalene with sodium and isoamyl alcohol gives:","options":["Tetralin","Decalin","1,4-Dihydronaphthalene","Naphthol"],"answer":0,"explain":"Na/isoamyl alcohol (higher temp) gives 1,2,3,4-tetrahydronaphthalene; Na/ethanol gives 1,4-dihydro."},{"id":"301M42","unit":5,"q":"According to Baeyer, angle strain (deviation) in cyclobutane is:","options":["24 deg 44'","9 deg 44'","0 deg 44'","-5 deg 16'"],"answer":1,"explain":"d = 1/2 (109 deg 28' - 90 deg) = 9 deg 44'."},{"id":"301M43","unit":5,"q":"Baeyer's strain theory predicted the most stable ring to be:","options":["Cyclopropane","Cyclobutane","Cyclopentane","Cyclohexane"],"answer":2,"explain":"Cyclopentane has the smallest deviation (0 deg 44') for a planar ring."},{"id":"301M44","unit":5,"q":"The heat of combustion per CH2 of cyclohexane is about equal to that of:","options":["Cyclopropane","Cyclobutane","Benzene","Open-chain alkanes"],"answer":3,"explain":"About 157.4 kcal/mol per CH2, showing cyclohexane is strain-free."},{"id":"301M45","unit":5,"q":"Bent or 'banana' bonds in cyclopropane were proposed by:","options":["Coulson and Moffitt","Baeyer","Sachse and Mohr","Huckel"],"answer":0,"explain":"Interorbital angle about 104 deg; orbitals overlap outside the C-C axis."},{"id":"301M46","unit":5,"q":"Cyclopropane reacts with HBr to give:","options":["Bromocyclopropane","1-Bromopropane","2-Bromopropane","1,3-Dibromopropane"],"answer":1,"explain":"HBr opens the strained ring giving n-propyl bromide."},{"id":"301M47","unit":5,"q":"Catalytic hydrogenation of cyclobutane to n-butane requires about:","options":["Room temperature","80 C","200 C","Does not occur"],"answer":2,"explain":"Cyclopropane opens at about 80 C; less strained cyclobutane needs about 200 C."},{"id":"301M48","unit":5,"q":"Which statement about cyclohexane is correct?","options":["It is planar with 120 deg angles","Boat form is more stable than chair","It has 9 axial hydrogens","Chair form has all bonds staggered"],"answer":3,"explain":"Chair: staggered bonds, no angle or torsional strain; 6 axial and 6 equatorial H."},{"id":"301M49","unit":5,"q":"Freund's method for cyclopropane uses:","options":["1,3-Dibromopropane + Zn","Propene + CH2I2","Calcium adipate","Diethyl malonate + ethylene"],"answer":0,"explain":"Intramolecular Wurtz reaction of 1,3-dibromopropane with zinc."},{"id":"301M50","unit":5,"q":"Which reagent distinguishes cyclopropane from propene?","options":["Br2 in CCl4","Cold dilute alkaline KMnO4","H2/Ni","Combustion"],"answer":1,"explain":"Propene decolourises Baeyer's reagent; cyclopropane does not."}]};
